  "lessons": [
    {
      "key": "lesson-14",
      "name": "Pre-Assessment",
      "absolute_position": 1,
      "lockable": true,
      "has_lesson_plan": false,
      "relative_position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "assessment": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-14",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-3",
        "script.name": "aif-year2-semester1-unit5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-1",
      "name": "What is Natural Language Processing?",
      "absolute_position": 2,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-user-graduate\"></i> ***Career Journeys***\n\n**Why is it challenging for computers to process language?**\n\nIn this lesson, students learn about natural language processing (NLP) of the English language by exploring three of its applications: speech recognition, language understanding, and text classification. Each surfaces a variation on the central challenge of NLP: the ambiguity of language.",
        "preparation": "- Print copies of the Natural Language Processing Unit Guide (one for each student)",
        "student_overview": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-user-graduate\"></i> ***Career Journeys***\n\n**Why is it challenging for computers to process language?**\n\nIn this lesson, you'll learn about natural language processing (NLP) of the English language by exploring three of its applications: speech recognition, language understanding, and text classification. Each surfaces a variation on the central challenge of NLP: the ambiguity of language."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-1",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "aif-year2-semester1-unit5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-2",
      "name": "Tokenization",
      "absolute_position": 3,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How does a computer represent written English?**\n\nIn this lesson, students become familiar with tokens, the building blocks of many languages in natural language processing. Students come to see tokenization as a necessary step towards analyzing and generating language, using tokens to creatively construct their own poetry. Students also learn how to tokenize text in Python, becoming familiar with the `split` string method, the basics of regular expressions, and the capabilities of `re`—Python’s regular expression module.",
        "preparation": "- Provide, or ask students to bring, blank sheets of paper",
        "student_overview": "**How does a computer represent written English?**\n\nIn this lesson, you'll become familiar with tokens, the building blocks of many languages in natural language processing."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-2",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "aif-year2-semester1-unit5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-3",
      "name": "Cleaning Text",
      "absolute_position": 4,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-user-graduate\"></i> ***Career Journeys***\n\n**How should text be prepared for use in AI?**\n\nIn this lesson, students learn about cleaning text by taking on the role of archivists at a library. They clean a catalog filled with messy data. Students first learn why text normalization is important in natural language processing, then practice applying normalization rules to the catalog by hand. Finally, students translate text normalization rules into Python code.",
        "preparation": "- Print copies of the Volcano Catalog Activity Guide (one for each student).\n- Provide, or ask students to bring, pens or pencils.",
        "student_overview": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-user-graduate\"></i> ***Career Journeys***\n\n**How should text be prepared for use in AI?**\n\nIn this lesson, you'll learn about cleaning text by taking on the role of archivists at a library. You'll learn why text normalization is important in natural language processing, practice applying normalization rules, and translate text normalization rules into Python code."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-3",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "aif-year2-semester1-unit5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-4",
      "name": "Frequency Analysis",
      "absolute_position": 5,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**What kinds of analysis can be performed on text?**\n\nIn this lesson, students encounter frequency analysis as a technique for finding the information someone is looking for. Exploring how a search engine might be implemented, students analyze the frequencies of words in multiple websites by hand, recommending which is most relevant to a user’s query. Students then write Python programs to implement these ideas in code.",
        "preparation": "- Print copies of the Frequency Analysis Activity Guide (one for each student).",
        "student_overview": "**What kinds of analysis can be performed on text?**\n\nIn this lesson, you'll encounter frequency analysis as a technique for finding the information someone is looking for. Exploring how a search engine might be implemented, you'll analyze the frequencies of words in multiple websites and write code to recommend a relevant page."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-4",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "aif-year2-semester1-unit5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-5",
      "name": "Keyword Extraction",
      "absolute_position": 6,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How can I tell what’s important in a piece of text?**\n\nIn this lesson, students build on frequency analysis to understand tf-idf, a measure of how important a word is to a document. Working for a video website that needs to know what each of a cooking channel’s videos is about, they score three candidate keywords for one transcript by hand, computing term frequency, document frequency, inverse document frequency and tf-idf. Then they complete a Python program that scores every word of every transcript, writing the term frequency and inverse document frequency functions themselves.",
        "preparation": "- Print copies of the Video Keywords Activity Guide (one for each student)\n- Provide, or ask students to bring, calculators\n",
        "student_overview": "**How can I tell what’s important in a piece of text?**\n\nIn this lesson, you'll build on frequency analysis to understand tf-idf, a measure of how important a word is to a document."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-5",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "aif-year2-semester1-unit5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-6",
      "name": "Text Classifiers",
      "absolute_position": 7,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How can AI be used to classify text?**\n\nIn this lesson, students explore how to build a machine learning model with text as the data. Students learn about text classification through the lens of spam filtering. They start by looking at spam text messages and brainstorming features that could be used to identify whether a message is spam or not. Then, they write Python programs to extract the relevant features from text and use them in a decision tree classifier to predict spam. After evaluating the classifier’s accuracy, they have an opportunity to add new features to improve the accuracy of their model.\n",
        "preparation": "- Provide, or ask students to bring, blank sheets of paper.",
        "student_overview": "**How can AI be used to classify text?**\n\nIn this lesson, you'll explore how to build a machine learning model with text as the data. You'll learn about text classification through the lens of spam filtering. You'll look at spam text messages and brainstorming features that could be used to identify whether a message is spam or not, then write Python programs to extract the relevant features from text and use them in a decision tree classifier to predict spam. "
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-6",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "aif-year2-semester1-unit5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-7",
      "name": "Bag of Words",
      "absolute_position": 8,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How can computers make sense of lots of text?**\n\nIn this lesson, students explore the bag of words model as an approach to representing language. Through a series of discussions involving language in different contexts, students see how the bag of words model can capture information about text and be used to make predictions. Then, students write Python code to compute bag of words features to implement classifiers that work with text.",
        "student_overview": "**How can computers make sense of lots of text?**\n\nIn this lesson, you'll explore the bag of words model as an approach to representing language. Through a series of discussions involving language in different contexts, you'll see how the bag of words model can capture information about text and be used to make predictions. Then, you'll write Python code to compute bag of words features to implement classifiers that work with text."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-7",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "aif-year2-semester1-unit5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-8",
      "name": "Naive Bayes",
      "absolute_position": 9,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**What patterns in language can I use to make predictions?**\n\nIn this lesson, students learn about Naive Bayes as an approach to classifying text. First, students see conditional probability as a tool for evaluating the likelihood of an event given existing data. They use conditional probability to analyze product reviews and conduct sentiment analysis. Then, students write Python code to train a Naive Bayes classifier and experiment with their classifier to see how it responds to different inputs.",
        "preparation": "- Print copies of the Product Reviews Activity Guide (one for each pair of students)\n- Provide, or ask students to bring, calculators",
        "student_overview": "**What patterns in language can I use to make predictions?**\n\nIn this lesson, you'll learn about Naive Bayes as an approach to classifying text. You'll use conditional probability to analyze product reviews and conduct sentiment analysis. Then, you'll write Python code to train a Naive Bayes classifier and experiment with their classifier to see how it responds to different inputs."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-8",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "aif-year2-semester1-unit5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-9",
      "name": "N-Grams",
      "absolute_position": 10,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**What can computers learn from text sequences?**\n\nIn this lesson, students explore how sequences of words can carry meaning beyond what individual words convey on their own. Students learn about n-grams, sequences of consecutive tokens, and practice finding bigrams—sequences of two tokens—in text by hand. They then translate these ideas into code, finishing two Python programs that find bigrams and n-grams in a piece of text.",
        "preparation": "- Provide, or ask students to bring, sheets of paper",
        "student_overview": "**What can computers learn from text sequences?**\n\nIn this lesson, you'll explore how sequences of words can carry meaning beyond what individual words convey on their own."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-9",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "aif-year2-semester1-unit5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-10",
      "name": "Markov Chains",
      "absolute_position": 11,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-user-graduate\"></i> ***Career Journeys***\n\n**How can AI generate text?**\n\nIn this lesson, students revisit their Unit 1 exploration of autocomplete—a technology that suggests what a user means to type. Students leverage their understanding of tokens and the previous lesson’s bigrams to build a simple language model: one that predicts the next word based on the last word a user has typed.",
        "preparation": "- Print one copy of the Be the Autocomplete Activity Guide for each student\n- Bring one die for each student\n",
        "student_overview": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-user-graduate\"></i> ***Career Journeys***\n\n**How can AI generate text?**\n\nIn this lesson, you'll revisit your Unit 1 exploration of autocomplete—a technology that suggests what a user means to type."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-10",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "aif-year2-semester1-unit5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-11",
      "name": "Limits of Natural Language Processing",
      "absolute_position": 12,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-user-graduate\"></i> ***Career Journeys***\n\n**What are the limits of text-based AI systems?**\n\nIn this lesson, students consider natural language AI systems and how they might fail. Students start by taking on the role of fact-checkers at a newspaper. They verify AI-generated headlines against the school newspaper’s record of events, coming to understand a key limitation of language models: hallucinations, which can become misinformation if treated as true. Students also explore how natural language systems can be exploited, considering how people might take advantage of what a language model has learned to produce undesirable results.",
        "preparation": "- Print copies of the Limits of Language Models Activity Guide (one for each student; two pages)",
        "student_overview": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-user-graduate\"></i> ***Career Journeys***\n\n**What are the limits of text-based AI systems?**\n\nIn this lesson, you'll consider natural language AI systems and how they might fail."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-11",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "aif-year2-semester1-unit5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-12",
      "name": "Text Corpora",
      "absolute_position": 13,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**Why does it matter what text data is used to train AI?**\n\nIn this lesson, students explore questions related to the ethics of natural language processing AI models. Students consider the use case of AI used to evaluate job applicants, considering how a model might be trained on a corpus of text data and evaluating the kinds of predictions such a model might make. Then, students engage in discussion across a range of different ethical questions to consider how language-based AI can be used fairly and responsibly.\n",
        "preparation": "- Print copies of the Job Applications Activity Guide (one for each pair of students)",
        "student_overview": "**Why does it matter what text data is used to train AI?**\n\nIn this lesson, you'll explore questions related to the ethics of natural language processing AI models. You'll consider the use case of AI used to evaluate job applicants, considering how a model might be trained on a corpus of text data and evaluating the kinds of predictions such a model might make. Then, you'll engage in discussion across a range of different ethical questions to consider how language-based AI can be used fairly and responsibly.\n",
        "unplugged": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-12",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "aif-year2-semester1-unit5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-13",
      "name": "Text Generation Project",
      "absolute_position": 14,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "In this lesson, students build a bigram language model to generate sentences. Students first choose from a selection of corpora with which to build their model and learn how to contribute their own sentences to their chosen corpus. They then implement their bigram language model by completing a tokenizing function, a training function, a generating function, and a detokenizing function. Finally, students explore how to evaluate the outputs of generative models, adopt their own metrics with which to evaluate their model, and conclude by assessing how well their model performs on their chosen metrics.",
        "preparation": "- Print one copy of the Text Generation Project Planning Guide for each student. ",
        "student_overview": "In this lesson, you'll build a bigram language model to generate sentences."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-13",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "aif-year2-semester1-unit5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-15",
      "name": "End of Unit Assessment",
