  "activity_sections": [
    {
      "key": "4712de3a-d914-471c-92d0-a8efb687cb43",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Pre-Assessment"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4712de3a-d914-471c-92d0-a8efb687cb43",
        "lesson_activity.key": "218ed1f5-54a2-4b1a-82bd-c41253d07aa2"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0d966692-3c17-43c5-89a1-41f3f3189871",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Have you ever talked to a computer, a smartphone, or a chatbot? What was your experience?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Surface students’ prior experiences with natural language processing. Students might mention talking to a voice assistant—Siri, Alexa, or the like. They might also have talked, in voice or in writing, to a chatbot—Claude, ChatGPT, Gemini, Copilot, or similar. If students have difficulty recalling examples like these, remind them that they’ve likely \"talked\" to AI Tutor in Python Lab. As students share examples, invite them to share their thoughts on the experience. Was the interaction smooth or stilted? Did everything go the way they wanted? The ultimate goal of the discussion is to gather a wide range of examples that depict the many ways humans interact with computers using natural language."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0d966692-3c17-43c5-89a1-41f3f3189871",
        "lesson_activity.key": "57d44437-e521-4f7f-aeb1-3fb3034adccc"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4497233f-1de5-42f6-9c3b-3b8eccdf7196",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "This unit is all about how technologies like the ones we’ve discussed actually work. Let’s start exploring!",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4497233f-1de5-42f6-9c3b-3b8eccdf7196",
        "lesson_activity.key": "57d44437-e521-4f7f-aeb1-3fb3034adccc"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "241ea679-8ed0-4cfe-868d-47182fb5a975",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the Natural Language Processing Unit Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *natural language processing*."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "241ea679-8ed0-4cfe-868d-47182fb5a975",
        "lesson_activity.key": "57d44437-e521-4f7f-aeb1-3fb3034adccc"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f8500cc9-16a6-4706-9386-6f4b546ec8f8",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "This unit focuses on a field of AI called \"natural language processing.\" The goal of natural language processing is to enable computers to use language in ways that are similar to the way we do. But as we’ll see today, natural language processing comes with a unique set of challenges. We’ll explore some of the many things natural language processing is used for and, along the way, see some of the unique challenges it faces.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f8500cc9-16a6-4706-9386-6f4b546ec8f8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "57d44437-e521-4f7f-aeb1-3fb3034adccc"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8e6aa229-45cf-4bfa-b340-24195042391c",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce this lesson’s career connection: a computational linguist."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8e6aa229-45cf-4bfa-b340-24195042391c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "57d44437-e521-4f7f-aeb1-3fb3034adccc"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "88140bd2-49e7-4565-b8ba-051a3cc86dee",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Computational linguists are interdisciplinary workers who leverage ideas from computer science, machine learning, AI, and linguistics—the study of language. They’re often at the forefront of innovations in natural language processing.",
        "remarks": true,
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "The photo on the slide is of Emily Bender, a computational linguist."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "88140bd2-49e7-4565-b8ba-051a3cc86dee",
        "lesson_activity.key": "57d44437-e521-4f7f-aeb1-3fb3034adccc"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "693ef4dd-8e50-4d42-8acf-56b11a94e5ae",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "693ef4dd-8e50-4d42-8acf-56b11a94e5ae",
        "lesson_activity.key": "57d44437-e521-4f7f-aeb1-3fb3034adccc"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "50cc388d-5b5f-4d69-86c5-63dab16b1700",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Introduce speech recognition as an application of natural language processing, alongside the challenges that come with it.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce speech recognition as an application of natural language processing.",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Speech Recognition"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "50cc388d-5b5f-4d69-86c5-63dab16b1700",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4835e6b0-61e5-4db9-ab2d-cbef3f68a1e1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9e1ae64e-8008-4a3c-9cfd-3213e3f7b601",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "The first application of natural language processing we’ll consider today is speech recognition. Speech recognition is about identifying what a person is saying. When you talk, verbally, to a computer, speech recognition is the very first problem that needs to be solved. Without speech recognition, a computer won’t be able to understand you.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9e1ae64e-8008-4a3c-9cfd-3213e3f7b601",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4835e6b0-61e5-4db9-ab2d-cbef3f68a1e1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f938b21d-3165-4387-b9da-44567745a447",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Preview one of the challenges associated with speech recognition."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f938b21d-3165-4387-b9da-44567745a447",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4835e6b0-61e5-4db9-ab2d-cbef3f68a1e1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b0a55386-8eda-4646-af97-689b46c24207",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Speech recognition is a uniquely challenging task. Hard, even, for us humans! Let’s give it a try.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b0a55386-8eda-4646-af97-689b46c24207",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4835e6b0-61e5-4db9-ab2d-cbef3f68a1e1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a7009136-108b-45d9-afc4-2dd822a22901",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the first speech recognition challenge."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a7009136-108b-45d9-afc4-2dd822a22901",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4835e6b0-61e5-4db9-ab2d-cbef3f68a1e1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f3744110-a616-449c-b215-c9081f631081",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "I’ll play a clip of someone saying a phrase. Your job is to remember what you hear.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f3744110-a616-449c-b215-c9081f631081",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4835e6b0-61e5-4db9-ab2d-cbef3f68a1e1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3b0e6de6-1047-4f73-8ae9-830277719017",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Play the clip once. Then, play it a few more times until students have made up their minds.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Invite a few students to share what they think they heard. Consider prompting for additional context or ask them to exaggeratedly enunciate. You’ll want to make sure you’ve heard them correctly, since the whole point is that the phrase’s meaning is ambiguous! If some students disagree on what they’ve heard, that’s great—and part of the fun.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Reveal the two possible phrases that could have been spoken."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3b0e6de6-1047-4f73-8ae9-830277719017",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4835e6b0-61e5-4db9-ab2d-cbef3f68a1e1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f61446c4-6322-4c54-a112-7e527cd9d8f7",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Are you sure you heard \"Take a nice cold shower\"? Or is it possible they said \"Take an ice-cold shower\"? Let’s try a few more.",
        "remarks": true,
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "The examples in this Speech Recognition section are called \"oronyms\"—phrases that sound the same as, or similar to, other phrases. These examples come from https://linguisticsgirl.com/fun-with-english-oronyms, accessed September 17, 2026."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f61446c4-6322-4c54-a112-7e527cd9d8f7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4835e6b0-61e5-4db9-ab2d-cbef3f68a1e1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d682a41f-1dcf-4368-add8-adc36a10019c",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the second speech recognition challenge.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Repeat the protocol from the first example. Play the clip a few times. Then, invite a few students to share what they think they heard.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Reveal the two possible phrases that could have been spoken."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d682a41f-1dcf-4368-add8-adc36a10019c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4835e6b0-61e5-4db9-ab2d-cbef3f68a1e1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "49430b65-d251-40d0-8c1f-5d4dba9aef0d",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Did you hear \"The farmer’s outstanding in her field?\" Or, \"The farmer’s out standing in her field?\"",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "49430b65-d251-40d0-8c1f-5d4dba9aef0d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4835e6b0-61e5-4db9-ab2d-cbef3f68a1e1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "28c8c1cc-fbe1-4cc5-82ee-8e5930723ec2",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the third speech recognition challenge.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Repeat the protocol. Play the clip a few times. Then, invite a few a students to share what they think they heard.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Reveal the two possible phrases that could have been spoken."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "28c8c1cc-fbe1-4cc5-82ee-8e5930723ec2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4835e6b0-61e5-4db9-ab2d-cbef3f68a1e1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2603d15e-edbf-49e7-b3e4-d4b0e4d96354",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Did you hear \"The teacher taught sixty-six students today?\" Or, \"The teacher taught sixty sick students today?\"",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2603d15e-edbf-49e7-b3e4-d4b0e4d96354",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4835e6b0-61e5-4db9-ab2d-cbef3f68a1e1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b5a85e15-1ca1-4421-9f29-391058415ef2",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Prompt students to name the challenge they just experienced by completing the sentence. Gather a few responses, then reveal the takeaway."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b5a85e15-1ca1-4421-9f29-391058415ef2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4835e6b0-61e5-4db9-ab2d-cbef3f68a1e1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6b5aef1f-ff66-4a41-89df-f0851a5a4593",
      "position": 14,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Prompt students to name the challenge they just experienced by completing the sentence. Gather a few responses, then reveal the takeaway.",
        "remarks": true,
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "This activity focused on the English language. Consider asking students who know other languages to share their experiences with that language, including whether this challenge exists in their language too."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6b5aef1f-ff66-4a41-89df-f0851a5a4593",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4835e6b0-61e5-4db9-ab2d-cbef3f68a1e1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f09863a3-b058-4555-aadd-a4620c1b24ea",
      "position": 15,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Introduce language understanding as an application of natural language processing, alongside the challenges that come with it.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce language understanding as an application of natural language processing.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Preview one of the challenges associated with language understanding.",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Language Understanding"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f09863a3-b058-4555-aadd-a4620c1b24ea",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4835e6b0-61e5-4db9-ab2d-cbef3f68a1e1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "25e0d942-efa8-4279-975b-f6a946d9e8b3",
      "position": 16,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Even if a computer correctly recognizes speech, there are still plenty of challenges that come with understanding that speech. Let’s have a look.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "25e0d942-efa8-4279-975b-f6a946d9e8b3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4835e6b0-61e5-4db9-ab2d-cbef3f68a1e1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6a82c8b8-0bf0-4312-ae6f-be9ce6aaeabf",
      "position": 17,
      "properties": {
        "description": "In just a moment, we’ll look at a few pieces of text to understand the meaning of. First, find a partner who can help you with this task.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6a82c8b8-0bf0-4312-ae6f-be9ce6aaeabf",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4835e6b0-61e5-4db9-ab2d-cbef3f68a1e1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "dc659bb9-1889-4dcc-b617-8af8de9b05ca",
      "position": 18,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Pair students.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "For time’s sake, consider simply having students work with a partner already sitting near them."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "dc659bb9-1889-4dcc-b617-8af8de9b05ca",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4835e6b0-61e5-4db9-ab2d-cbef3f68a1e1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d1fd7735-2141-4abf-a491-aa05bda59d26",
      "position": 19,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the first language understanding challenge."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d1fd7735-2141-4abf-a491-aa05bda59d26",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4835e6b0-61e5-4db9-ab2d-cbef3f68a1e1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "bd6fac49-fbf1-43e3-a752-8fbee7c1db4f",
      "position": 20,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Suppose you get this text from a friend.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "bd6fac49-fbf1-43e3-a752-8fbee7c1db4f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4835e6b0-61e5-4db9-ab2d-cbef3f68a1e1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f183f21a-39d7-4c0c-8204-603883ed6314",
      "position": 21,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Prompt students to consider valid interpretations of the text."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f183f21a-39d7-4c0c-8204-603883ed6314",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4835e6b0-61e5-4db9-ab2d-cbef3f68a1e1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "21a4dde9-b249-4c97-9c8f-2b197a1cec8e",
      "position": 22,
      "properties": {
        "description": "And suppose an AI system is tasked with figuring out what this text means—that is, interpreting it. Work with your partner to find all the possible meanings of this text.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "21a4dde9-b249-4c97-9c8f-2b197a1cec8e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4835e6b0-61e5-4db9-ab2d-cbef3f68a1e1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c923df92-4d58-4bce-ba7b-4df3589a2fd5",
      "position": 23,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Once students have had a minute or two to think, gather interpretations from around the room. Competing interpretations are especially useful for supporting the main point of this activity.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Reveal three possible interpretations of the text."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c923df92-4d58-4bce-ba7b-4df3589a2fd5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4835e6b0-61e5-4db9-ab2d-cbef3f68a1e1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a6843c97-c5bf-4fac-a5ae-c64a4facbc00",
      "position": 24,
      "properties": {
        "description": "There are at least a few ways to interpret what your friend said. They could mean they really love cats. Or that they love big cats, specifically. Or, though more unlikely, that they consider themselves to be a big person who loves cats.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a6843c97-c5bf-4fac-a5ae-c64a4facbc00",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4835e6b0-61e5-4db9-ab2d-cbef3f68a1e1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2cfd0234-9def-4914-838f-a66a9336ae5e",
      "position": 25,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the second language understanding challenge. Follow the protocol from before. Prompt students, in pairs, to consider valid interpretations of the text. Then, gather interpretations from around the room. Again, competing interpretations are what you’re looking for. Finally, reveal possible interpretations of the text."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2cfd0234-9def-4914-838f-a66a9336ae5e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4835e6b0-61e5-4db9-ab2d-cbef3f68a1e1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c6a6db5a-e273-4a2d-bcbf-be3527a577bf",
      "position": 26,
      "properties": {
        "description": "There are at least two ways to interpret what this person said. They could mean that, when they found the dog, the dog had his favorite toy. Or, maybe the dog was lost and the person used the dog’s favorite toy to find him again.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c6a6db5a-e273-4a2d-bcbf-be3527a577bf",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4835e6b0-61e5-4db9-ab2d-cbef3f68a1e1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "cd5dc549-d48c-4c3e-8ca3-b3eba8accd3a",
      "position": 27,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the third language understanding challenge. Follow the protocol from before."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "cd5dc549-d48c-4c3e-8ca3-b3eba8accd3a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4835e6b0-61e5-4db9-ab2d-cbef3f68a1e1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "830dd904-49d7-46c6-ab70-6ae4d1e910a5",
      "position": 28,
      "properties": {
        "description": "There are at least two ways to interpret what this person said. They could mean that their teacher, who teaches ancient history, is retiring. Or, maybe they’re rudely implying their history teacher is ancient and really needs to retire.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "830dd904-49d7-46c6-ab70-6ae4d1e910a5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4835e6b0-61e5-4db9-ab2d-cbef3f68a1e1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8308f2da-9377-4856-98af-c638bb756a6f",
      "position": 29,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the fourth language understanding challenge. Follow the protocol from before.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8308f2da-9377-4856-98af-c638bb756a6f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4835e6b0-61e5-4db9-ab2d-cbef3f68a1e1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e416ade0-c085-4407-9ee3-0c0c5dd3c689",
      "position": 30,
      "properties": {
        "description": "There are at least two ways to interpret what this person said. It could be that Sara found the missing cookies. Or, it could be implied that Sara was the one who stole them, since they were found near her. Maybe both are true!",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e416ade0-c085-4407-9ee3-0c0c5dd3c689",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4835e6b0-61e5-4db9-ab2d-cbef3f68a1e1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d0f03845-071e-42a9-bd25-504f86636c37",
      "position": 31,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Prompt students to name the challenge they just experienced by completing the sentence. Gather a few responses, then reveal the takeaway."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d0f03845-071e-42a9-bd25-504f86636c37",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4835e6b0-61e5-4db9-ab2d-cbef3f68a1e1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c89728de-b3fc-46e1-b45f-8b5ec7fa17fd",
      "position": 32,
      "properties": {
        "description": "One of the reasons natural language processing—particularly language understanding—is difficult is that the same words can have different interpretations.",
        "remarks": true,
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "This activity focused on the English language. Consider again asking students who know other languages to share their experiences with that language, including whether this challenge exists in their language too."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c89728de-b3fc-46e1-b45f-8b5ec7fa17fd",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4835e6b0-61e5-4db9-ab2d-cbef3f68a1e1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6064d3f1-0db8-42c9-a8a3-0dbf29d4a88a",
      "position": 33,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Introduce text classification as an application of natural language processing, alongside the challenges that come with it.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce text classification as an application of natural language processing.",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Text Classification"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6064d3f1-0db8-42c9-a8a3-0dbf29d4a88a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4835e6b0-61e5-4db9-ab2d-cbef3f68a1e1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8d0965df-ae16-46e7-a12c-76050ae5b993",
      "position": 34,
      "properties": {
        "description": "The third application of natural language processing we’ll consider today is text classification. It’s useful when you want to categorize text. Email spam filters are a classic example of text classification: they read text, then decide whether it’s spam or not spam.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8d0965df-ae16-46e7-a12c-76050ae5b993",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4835e6b0-61e5-4db9-ab2d-cbef3f68a1e1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8ee2430b-a2b7-4a78-a430-33bfee75f323",
      "position": 35,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Preview one of the challenges associated with text classification."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8ee2430b-a2b7-4a78-a430-33bfee75f323",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4835e6b0-61e5-4db9-ab2d-cbef3f68a1e1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "83bcf4e4-0ae0-4fec-af6a-4dd50e88bf15",
      "position": 36,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Here, too, computers can run into difficulty with the nature of language. Let’s take a look.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "83bcf4e4-0ae0-4fec-af6a-4dd50e88bf15",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4835e6b0-61e5-4db9-ab2d-cbef3f68a1e1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "73892c56-511f-4258-87aa-dfa3cff7c0e0",
      "position": 37,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Prompt students to get into groups of three.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Prompt students to work together to classify the given phrases as sarcastic or genuine.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work, consider defining sarcasm by example, so all students have a reference to work from. However, avoid saying the specific phrases on the slide out loud: the point, as students will later see, is that it’s nearly impossible to tell whether these phrases are sarcastic or genuine without some information about the tone in which they were delivered.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "For time’s sake, students could also do this activity in pairs, with their same partner from the prior activity."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "73892c56-511f-4258-87aa-dfa3cff7c0e0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4835e6b0-61e5-4db9-ab2d-cbef3f68a1e1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "db68fda3-2f05-446e-9b78-4f66265a742e",
      "position": 38,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *How did you decide which phrases were sarcastic?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Prompt students to share their thinking from the classification exercise. Strong responses will say that, actually, it’s hard to tell whether these phrases are sarcastic or genuine. It’s true that a couple phrases, such as \"What a surprise,\" are more often than not said in a sarcastic manner. But there’s nothing in the phrase itself to suggest whether it’s to be construed sarcastically or genuinely. Make the point that a classifier can’t rely on prior experience like a human can."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "db68fda3-2f05-446e-9b78-4f66265a742e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4835e6b0-61e5-4db9-ab2d-cbef3f68a1e1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ed3bc02b-0093-46cf-ba37-6ed67e0550d9",
      "position": 39,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Turns out it’s a difficult, if not impossible, task given the phrases alone. Let’s see why.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ed3bc02b-0093-46cf-ba37-6ed67e0550d9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4835e6b0-61e5-4db9-ab2d-cbef3f68a1e1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "85f4c2c2-d972-4cdb-93bd-e62b52f1bd3e",
      "position": 40,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Guide students in saying each of the phrases in a sarcastic tone. Then, in a genuine tone. The goal is to help students realize that tone almost entirely determines whether a phrase is construed as sarcastic or genuine—information a computer doesn’t have easy access to.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "This activity could be a  call-and-response activity, if it works for your classroom."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "85f4c2c2-d972-4cdb-93bd-e62b52f1bd3e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4835e6b0-61e5-4db9-ab2d-cbef3f68a1e1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "99ddbf2b-0d80-42a7-92f0-94163c293612",
      "position": 41,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Prompt students to name the challenge they just experienced by completing the sentence. Gather a few responses, then reveal the takeaway."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "99ddbf2b-0d80-42a7-92f0-94163c293612",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4835e6b0-61e5-4db9-ab2d-cbef3f68a1e1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7f6a3051-f02e-4180-a063-c59b9852946c",
      "position": 42,
      "properties": {
        "description": "One of the reasons natural language processing—particularly text classification—is difficult is that information comes from tone, not just text.",
        "remarks": true,
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Astute students might suggest that tone, like text, could be encoded and therefore be used as a feature in a classification task. In fact, modern language models are capable of similar feats today, with varying degrees of success. This challenge also remains an open area of research for computational linguists."
          },
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "This lesson focuses on the English language. In other languages, tone may be indicated by the text of a word itself."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7f6a3051-f02e-4180-a063-c59b9852946c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4835e6b0-61e5-4db9-ab2d-cbef3f68a1e1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "52f1b501-416f-4785-bdb4-c2e8319cdb26",
      "position": 43,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define ambiguity."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "52f1b501-416f-4785-bdb4-c2e8319cdb26",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4835e6b0-61e5-4db9-ab2d-cbef3f68a1e1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "963a5de4-1e02-41d9-a96c-16b6f595fb0a",
      "position": 44,
      "properties": {
        "description": "All of the challenges we’ve explored today are really variations on a single challenge: ambiguity. Language is inherently ambiguous, changing its meaning over time and across contexts. That’s one of the core reasons natural language processing needs machine learning to be successful.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "963a5de4-1e02-41d9-a96c-16b6f595fb0a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4835e6b0-61e5-4db9-ab2d-cbef3f68a1e1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8c0eb535-0474-41e2-924d-068a71065a5d",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Check Your Understanding"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8c0eb535-0474-41e2-924d-068a71065a5d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "415f4a8d-477c-43a9-92dd-b76c0b761b07"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "57a0edcd-d11f-47cf-9d2f-0c19d92eaf8d",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 1, Level 1, to answer the Question of the Day.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "57a0edcd-d11f-47cf-9d2f-0c19d92eaf8d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "415f4a8d-477c-43a9-92dd-b76c0b761b07"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "31665f25-fb57-46df-a486-5c6daa6b1fa1",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Briefly introduce students to tokens.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** As students enter, direct them to find and sit with a partner for the Token Poetry activity.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Distribute, or have students take out, a blank piece of paper.",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Token Poetry"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "31665f25-fb57-46df-a486-5c6daa6b1fa1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7bad7e0d-cfd2-4b7d-b65c-3b3bda8a3ef4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7869701d-8730-4059-b38e-84bf2512ef41",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Today we’ll continue exploring natural language processing. Let’s start by seeing language the way a computer might.",
        "remarks": true,
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "The Token Poetry activity is designed to be a quick, fun, and likely comedic hook. It shouldn’t take more than 8–10 minutes of class time. If it saves time in your classroom, students could complete the activity individually instead of in pairs."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7869701d-8730-4059-b38e-84bf2512ef41",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7bad7e0d-cfd2-4b7d-b65c-3b3bda8a3ef4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "fb992b74-2b8d-45fc-8bca-94e4c7c2784a",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *token*."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "fb992b74-2b8d-45fc-8bca-94e4c7c2784a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7bad7e0d-cfd2-4b7d-b65c-3b3bda8a3ef4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0db1171f-3acf-41cd-bb53-3bdd90a8261d",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "A bit like how you might read a sentence word by word, computers work with text token by token. In the context of natural language processing, a token is an individual unit of language. Tokens can come in many forms. For now you can think of them as words or punctuation. By creating tokens from text, a computer can better analyze text or recombine tokens into new phrases, sentences, or—as we’ll do now—poetry!",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0db1171f-3acf-41cd-bb53-3bdd90a8261d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7bad7e0d-cfd2-4b7d-b65c-3b3bda8a3ef4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1b7ae08f-9621-4c98-aa5d-074827302c64",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain the Token Poetry activity.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Share an example token poem, to illustrate what students will create.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Show the available tokens to students. Set a timer for three minutes, then let students create their token poems.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work, make sure they’re using only the tokens listed on the slide. They can’t use any additional punctuation, words, modifiers, etc.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Share your token poem. What did you create?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Invite a few students to share their token poems. Keep the sharing light-hearted and fun. Once a few students have shared their token poems, continue on."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1b7ae08f-9621-4c98-aa5d-074827302c64",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7bad7e0d-cfd2-4b7d-b65c-3b3bda8a3ef4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3589ae5c-04b5-4ac1-8061-21f94cc41779",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Now you’ve seen why computers use tokens: they’re units of language that can be combined and analyzed easily. But before we dive deeper into all that computers do with tokens, we first need to see how computers get tokens from text. That’s what we’ll focus on today.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3589ae5c-04b5-4ac1-8061-21f94cc41779",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7bad7e0d-cfd2-4b7d-b65c-3b3bda8a3ef4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "675b5128-7b27-4a8d-aed2-ac0cbd564a09",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "675b5128-7b27-4a8d-aed2-ac0cbd564a09",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7bad7e0d-cfd2-4b7d-b65c-3b3bda8a3ef4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2f91010a-ba73-4d5f-b4ca-e03ab49677a3",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Introduce students to tokenization: the process of extracting tokens from text.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *tokenization*.",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Tokenization"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2f91010a-ba73-4d5f-b4ca-e03ab49677a3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0ddef5b5-88f4-4044-938e-9a55dfa42b91"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7e7a8698-0c86-4992-9998-bcfdf9076aa3",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Before a computer can work with tokens, it needs to turn text into tokens. That process is called tokenization. There are many different ways to tokenize text. We’ll learn about a few today.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7e7a8698-0c86-4992-9998-bcfdf9076aa3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0ddef5b5-88f4-4044-938e-9a55dfa42b91"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4f7e5a83-9080-4970-bcbd-fb0757b30cca",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce `split` as a method of tokenization.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What do you think this program will do?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Prompt students to predict what the `split` method does. Students aren’t expected to know the right answer. Rather, the goal is to create a moment for students to practice their inference skills when reading new code.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Reveal what the program does."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4f7e5a83-9080-4970-bcbd-fb0757b30cca",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0ddef5b5-88f4-4044-938e-9a55dfa42b91"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "688481fe-dfef-478a-b0d6-e867e1357baf",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "One simple way to tokenize text is to use the `split` method. The `split` method splits text, by default, on whitespace. So from the text \"Hello world,\" for example, we could get two tokens: \"Hello\" and \"world.\"",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "688481fe-dfef-478a-b0d6-e867e1357baf",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0ddef5b5-88f4-4044-938e-9a55dfa42b91"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "117727c3-04d2-463d-91e3-384ce071da3b",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Let’s look more closely at the `split` method as an approach to tokenization.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "117727c3-04d2-463d-91e3-384ce071da3b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0ddef5b5-88f4-4044-938e-9a55dfa42b91"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "52247d22-6228-498f-8e30-c2a8ac36a67b",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Tokenization"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "52247d22-6228-498f-8e30-c2a8ac36a67b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0ddef5b5-88f4-4044-938e-9a55dfa42b91"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "dc36e448-1bbf-461a-aa8b-af3bb63ae5ee",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 2, Level 1.\n- **Level 1:** Students see another example of the `split` method in action. They predict what the program will do, then study the list of tokens it actually generates.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** This initial level is designed to be quick. As students look at how split created tokens from the given text, prompt them to consider any differences between what they see here and what they saw in the Token Poetry activity.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Using split, what became a token? What didn’t?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Prompt students to share their findings from studying the list of tokens their program generated. Strong responses will notice that split creates tokens with punctuation inside: punctuation isn’t separated into tokens of its own. For example, splitting the phrase \"Hello, world.\" would result in only two tokens: \"Hello,\" and \"world.\".\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Motivate another approach to tokenization that resolves the shortcomings of split."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "dc36e448-1bbf-461a-aa8b-af3bb63ae5ee",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0ddef5b5-88f4-4044-938e-9a55dfa42b91"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e7292f7b-1f22-4b7d-9042-89520daf6eb3",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "With `split` alone, punctuation isn’t tokenized by itself: it’s included as part of other tokens. This could be okay, but it also could create some problems: imagine trying to analyze text and finding different forms of \"Hello\": one with a period at the end, one with an exclamation mark at the end, etc. Additionally, words with punctuation aren’t nearly as flexible at being put together in new ways.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e7292f7b-1f22-4b7d-9042-89520daf6eb3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0ddef5b5-88f4-4044-938e-9a55dfa42b91"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1625f9f7-3d61-4fa2-acad-27b7036915d9",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "We need another approach—one that can tokenize punctuation separately.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1625f9f7-3d61-4fa2-acad-27b7036915d9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0ddef5b5-88f4-4044-938e-9a55dfa42b91"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "963f1634-7201-4446-ab1f-139c049db006",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Introduce students to regular expressions as an approach to tokenization.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *regular expression*.",
        "duration": "20",
        "name": "Regular Expressions"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "963f1634-7201-4446-ab1f-139c049db006",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0ddef5b5-88f4-4044-938e-9a55dfa42b91"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "cafc82f8-7c3d-4554-af6d-f833cd4dd04f",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "A regular expression is a sequence of symbols. These symbols give us great flexibility when tokenizing text. That’s because the symbols represent a pattern in text—and you can create all kinds of patterns to tokenize with! Some regular expressions will look like nonsense at first, but you’ll learn today how to read and use them.",
        "remarks": true,
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "This lesson also includes a supplementary video that you can use as an additional resource for students."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "cafc82f8-7c3d-4554-af6d-f833cd4dd04f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0ddef5b5-88f4-4044-938e-9a55dfa42b91"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e98dc83e-bb90-4c7c-905e-215109485152",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Briefly introduce regular expressions by example."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e98dc83e-bb90-4c7c-905e-215109485152",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0ddef5b5-88f4-4044-938e-9a55dfa42b91"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "305e5af7-b726-44e6-aa35-ad469a0b4035",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "description": "A regular expression defines a pattern and is often used to find text that matches that pattern. In Python, regular expressions are best placed in raw strings—a string with an r in front. A simple regular expression would be this one: `\"home\"`. These symbols can be read literally: they represent the pattern \"h,\" followed by \"o,\" followed by \"m,\" followed by \"e.\" Where do you see that pattern in the text? It’s highlighted for us.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "305e5af7-b726-44e6-aa35-ad469a0b4035",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0ddef5b5-88f4-4044-938e-9a55dfa42b91"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4b58a27d-2949-4805-b3cf-cc2618688f34",
      "position": 14,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce another simple regular expression that can be read literally."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4b58a27d-2949-4805-b3cf-cc2618688f34",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0ddef5b5-88f4-4044-938e-9a55dfa42b91"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b0537a81-3a87-4321-b739-6ca3cb6229ff",
      "position": 15,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Here’s another simple regular expression: `\"galaxy\"`. These symbols can again be read literally. Where do you see that pattern in the text? This time, there are two locations that pattern appears—they’re highlighted for us."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b0537a81-3a87-4321-b739-6ca3cb6229ff",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0ddef5b5-88f4-4044-938e-9a55dfa42b91"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4b39e6c3-a003-4ffb-9cc2-94f42845af59",
      "position": 16,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce a more complex regular expression that can’t be read literally. Rather than explain this particular expression, the goal of this brief introduction is to show students the power of regular expressions: a pattern that matches many different pieces of text can be expressed in only three symbols."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4b39e6c3-a003-4ffb-9cc2-94f42845af59",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0ddef5b5-88f4-4044-938e-9a55dfa42b91"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "812a93d2-bcae-48a7-9dc8-b47f2158fa44",
      "position": 17,
      "properties": {
        "description": "And here’s a more complex regular expression: `\"\\w+\"`. These symbols can’t be read literally: we aren’t searching for \"\\,\" followed by \"w,\" followed by \"+.\" Rather, these are special symbols that denote a more meaningful pattern. You don’t need to understand these symbols right now: you’ll learn how to do so in just a minute. For now, just notice the power of a regular expression: using only three symbols, we can represent a pattern that matches all of these sequences you see highlighted.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If looking for an engagement opportunity, you could consider pausing and asking: \"Based on the highlighted matches, what pattern would you guess these symbols represent?\" Plausible answers include variations on \"Unbroken sequences of letters or numbers.\""
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "812a93d2-bcae-48a7-9dc8-b47f2158fa44",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0ddef5b5-88f4-4044-938e-9a55dfa42b91"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b9244148-9b8a-41ba-a4cb-8dbd6717f901",
      "position": 18,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the `re` library and the role of regular expressions in tokenization."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b9244148-9b8a-41ba-a4cb-8dbd6717f901",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0ddef5b5-88f4-4044-938e-9a55dfa42b91"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3d9915f5-44a7-487f-a8eb-4cd7865a03e5",
      "position": 19,
      "properties": {
        "description": "To use regular expressions in Python, you’ll need the `re` library. The `re` library comes with functions like `findall`, which finds all of the matches to a pattern and returns them in a list. This is handy for tokenizing!",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3d9915f5-44a7-487f-a8eb-4cd7865a03e5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0ddef5b5-88f4-4044-938e-9a55dfa42b91"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2786aa36-73f4-4079-b7ea-86bc51b9c14f",
      "position": 20,
      "properties": {
        "description": "One of the best ways to understand regular expressions is to experiment with them. Now, you’ll do just that with the Tokenization Widget.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2786aa36-73f4-4079-b7ea-86bc51b9c14f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0ddef5b5-88f4-4044-938e-9a55dfa42b91"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6f219880-e0cc-4449-928a-dc73cc2df758",
      "position": 21,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Tokenization"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6f219880-e0cc-4449-928a-dc73cc2df758",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0ddef5b5-88f4-4044-938e-9a55dfa42b91"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "38841336-def1-4f5b-a93b-9a8e11ecfc2d",
      "position": 22,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Check Your Understanding"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "38841336-def1-4f5b-a93b-9a8e11ecfc2d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0ddef5b5-88f4-4044-938e-9a55dfa42b91"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7d6a7358-5502-4b6d-80e9-870cca5ed645",
      "position": 23,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 2, Levels 2–7.\n- **Level 2:** Students try a few simple, literal patterns in the Tokenization Widget.\n- **Level 3:** Students learn about character classes, such as `\\w` and `\\s`. They try them in the Tokenization Widget.\n- **Level 4:** Students learn about quantifiers—specifically `+`. They see how combining `\\w` with `+` can match unbroken sequences of word characters.\n- **Level 5:** Students learn about logic and character sets: `|`, `[]`, and `[^]`. They see how these tools help them construct a regular expression that tokenizes words and punctuation separately.\n- **Level 6:** Students implement the regular expression from Level 5 in Python, tokenizing given text.\n- **Level 7:** Students complete a Check Your Understanding.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work, look for…\n- Students to try all of the regular expressions they are prompted to try. If students skip some of the regular expressions they are prompted to try, their understanding of regular expressions may not develop as fully.\n- Students who could use additional explanation of the regular expressions they are prompted to try. To explain a more complex regular expression, break it down into individual symbols. For example, start with the character class (e.g., `\\w` or `\\s`) and explain the range of characters these symbols stand in for. Help students see that characters in this range are indeed shown as \"matched\" in the Tokenization Widget. Generally speaking, both verbally explaining an expression and showing its match results can help a student understand its meaning.\n- Students using `re.split` instead of `re.findall`. Explain that findall returns everything in the regular expression, while split returns everything not in the regular expression.\n- Students who finish early. Encourage them to try tokenizing a phrase like \"We should see will.i.am and Panic! At the Disco while we’re in the U.S.A.\"",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use Level 7 as an assessment opportunity, checking if a student has understood both the purposes of tokenization and the basics of regular expressions. Students are first prompted to describe the purpose of tokenization. Look for students to describe why tokenization is useful—not just what it is. Strong responses include variations of \"It makes text easier to analyze\" or \"It creates building blocks a computer can put together into longer sentences.\" Students are then prompted to put a regular expression into words of their own. Look for students to accurately describe the pattern it represents, or the results the pattern achieves when used with `findall`."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7d6a7358-5502-4b6d-80e9-870cca5ed645",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0ddef5b5-88f4-4044-938e-9a55dfa42b91"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1f7a7ced-7df5-4e97-a83c-07715ad48053",
      "position": 24,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Today you’ve explored how computers can represent written English. If you’re familiar with other languages, let’s consider what might need to be different.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1f7a7ced-7df5-4e97-a83c-07715ad48053",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0ddef5b5-88f4-4044-938e-9a55dfa42b91"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "108a06ce-4aaa-4da2-aa6a-54abef350ad9",
      "position": 25,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *How might a computer represent other languages differently?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Prompt students to consider how the tokenization process they’ve explored today might fail on languages other than English. A variety of responses can be strong here. Some students may point out that other languages—such as Japanese Kanji, Mandarin Chinese writing, etc.—are logographic: individual characters represent meaning, rather than collections of letters. Other students may point out that other languages—such as Spanish, French, etc.—contain a broader set of characters than English."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "108a06ce-4aaa-4da2-aa6a-54abef350ad9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0ddef5b5-88f4-4044-938e-9a55dfa42b91"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "085bce49-0068-48b7-8e41-21db800b25ce",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to answer the Question of the Day in their unit guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "085bce49-0068-48b7-8e41-21db800b25ce",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4fb7f622-b806-45f3-8473-0bbe18eabe05"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1381d884-b851-46fe-974c-f1a20de6b9b3",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce this lesson’s career connection: an archivist.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1381d884-b851-46fe-974c-f1a20de6b9b3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d6f8d411-9a59-4dde-835c-440f6526802b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "917c83ee-31b5-411c-9494-adbe18bbee12",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Today, you will take on the role of an archivist. An archivist organizes, preserves, and catalogs documents and records so other people can find them.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "917c83ee-31b5-411c-9494-adbe18bbee12",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d6f8d411-9a59-4dde-835c-440f6526802b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3b1fb273-2162-444b-b687-16821e20a9e9",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce students to the archivist’s catalog.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3b1fb273-2162-444b-b687-16821e20a9e9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d6f8d411-9a59-4dde-835c-440f6526802b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "991b2528-9c25-4849-a273-622cab2d6262",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Your task today will be to clean up an archivist’s catalog. A catalog is a list of books or other items that an organization, like a library, holds. Here, each entry represents a different item. Or do they?\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "991b2528-9c25-4849-a273-622cab2d6262",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d6f8d411-9a59-4dde-835c-440f6526802b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "915dceab-0d71-40e6-9035-bac57504e8f8",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Prompt students to examine the catalog.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *How many different items does this catalog really hold?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Give students an opportunity to notice that some entries, while typed differently, really represent the same item. Strong responses will point out four distinct items: \"Volcanoes\", \"The Volcano Book\", \"Volcanoes of the World\", and \"Volcanic Eruptions\". A student who says there are fewer than four items may have combined two titles that should have been distinct. A student who says there are five or more items may not have realized two differently-styled titles are actually the same."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "915dceab-0d71-40e6-9035-bac57504e8f8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d6f8d411-9a59-4dde-835c-440f6526802b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5c4877e2-2791-43b3-bcdb-9f1207d6a54e",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "This catalog seems to have been typed by many different people over many years, each with a different style. It takes a bit of effort to figure out how many items there really are, but it’s certainly doable! Let’s see how a computer could do the same.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5c4877e2-2791-43b3-bcdb-9f1207d6a54e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d6f8d411-9a59-4dde-835c-440f6526802b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ff88656e-6f51-46b1-8161-e70c32b71282",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce how a computer program would naively count the number of unique items.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *A computer program counted nine different items in the catalog. Why do you think it did so?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Prompt students to analyze why a computer program would treat each entry as a unique item. Strong responses will name the kinds of differences a person could learn to ignore but a computer, by default, would not: capital letters (e.g., \"Volcanoes\", \"volcanoes\", \"VOLCANOES\"), punctuation (e.g., the period after \"world\" in entry 3, the exclamation point in entry 4), and extra words (e.g., \"The Volcano Book” vs. \"volcano book\"). Write these three challenges on the board as they come up. If students don’t identify one or more of these three challenges, point to these differences in the catalog yourself."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ff88656e-6f51-46b1-8161-e70c32b71282",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d6f8d411-9a59-4dde-835c-440f6526802b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4c8d1d1c-e166-455d-a6f1-e2fc0b1fb125",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "While we might recognize that variations in capitalization, punctuation, and wording are irrelevant, a computer won’t. To a computer, an entry represents a different item unless it matches another entry exactly. Today, you’ll learn how to put these entries into a standard form, so that a program can see what you can see.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4c8d1d1c-e166-455d-a6f1-e2fc0b1fb125",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d6f8d411-9a59-4dde-835c-440f6526802b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "af2e1068-8f7e-414b-a80e-743702444acb",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "af2e1068-8f7e-414b-a80e-743702444acb",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d6f8d411-9a59-4dde-835c-440f6526802b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "efc5020d-f0a7-48b6-a7b1-d962f2b162f7",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Students standardize, by hand, the messy library catalog they’ve seen in the Warm Up. They learn and apply three rules in order to normalize the catalog’s entries.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the Volcano Catalog Activity Guide. Students work individually. It is fine for neighbors to compare answers.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Prompt students to read the introduction to the Activity Guide, as well as the three rules they’ll use to normalize the catalog’s entries.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Illustrate how to apply the three rules with a hypothetical example.",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Volcano Catalog"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "efc5020d-f0a7-48b6-a7b1-d962f2b162f7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "70cac4fc-e93e-4968-9dfa-1f4222424d13"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "25197e9f-eb3d-4e83-84c9-c5d37ca38a3b",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Here’s a hypothetical example of how to apply the three rules. First, lowercase all the letters. Then, remove any punctuation. Finally, remove the stop words—words like \"of\" and \"the\" that don’t carry much meaning.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "25197e9f-eb3d-4e83-84c9-c5d37ca38a3b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "70cac4fc-e93e-4968-9dfa-1f4222424d13"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c73e0506-2a7d-48af-9a43-96f940d8c85e",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Give students about five minutes to write the standard form of each entry, then fill in the table at the bottom of the page.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Watch for these student challenges:\n- Students applying the rules out of order, usually removing stop words before lowercasing, so “The” in entry 2 survives. Point at Rule 1: lowercase first, every time.\n- Students dropping “book” or “world” as if they were stop words. Only the seven printed words come out; nothing else does.\n- Students unsure what to count. The count is how many different standard forms appear, not how many entries. If two rows have the same standard form, they count once.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Reveal the standard forms. Have students check their Standard Form column against the slide and fix any entries that differ.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *The rules turned nine entries into four items. Did they merge anything that might really be two different books? And who decided these three rules?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Two points should surface. Normalization loses information: “Volcanoes” and “VOLCANOES!” became one item, though the exclamation point might mark a different book. The rules are choices made by people: the stop word list is an English list, so a Spanish title keeps its “del” and matches nothing. Whoever writes the rules decides what counts as the same."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c73e0506-2a7d-48af-9a43-96f940d8c85e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "70cac4fc-e93e-4968-9dfa-1f4222424d13"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d29645d5-a691-4bd8-8965-dfb95ff1d552",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Consider clarifying that the standard forms do not represent \"real titles.\" Rather, a standard form exists only for a program to compare entries, not for people to read. For example, a library would still print \"Volcanoes of the World\" on a book’s spine—even if the standard form of that book title is \"volcanoes world\".\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d29645d5-a691-4bd8-8965-dfb95ff1d552",
        "lesson_activity.key": "70cac4fc-e93e-4968-9dfa-1f4222424d13"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "287a743a-7041-4013-90f1-07a5f8f35a64",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define _normalization_.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "287a743a-7041-4013-90f1-07a5f8f35a64",
        "lesson_activity.key": "70cac4fc-e93e-4968-9dfa-1f4222424d13"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "de11f1b6-6125-47d2-af4e-3db77f8f4324",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "What you just did to the catalog has a name: normalization. Normalization is converting text into a standard form. This standard form is for the computer, not for the reader. A program compares standard forms to decide which entries are the same item. A standard form is a choice, not a fact. Someone decided which words to drop and which letters to keep. Every such choice can merge two different things or split one.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "de11f1b6-6125-47d2-af4e-3db77f8f4324",
        "lesson_activity.key": "70cac4fc-e93e-4968-9dfa-1f4222424d13"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ef185bf6-0c5c-425e-843f-6fda1837727b",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define _stop word_.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ef185bf6-0c5c-425e-843f-6fda1837727b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "70cac4fc-e93e-4968-9dfa-1f4222424d13"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6d4dc1e5-8872-4bfc-b34c-789a018e71fc",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Rule 3 removed stop words. A stop word is a common word, such as “the” or “and”, that may be removed from text before analysis because it carries little meaning. “The” rarely helps you tell one book from another. Removing them is a choice, not a law: sometimes a stop word is the meaning. “An apple a day” is advice; “Apple Day” is an event.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6d4dc1e5-8872-4bfc-b34c-789a018e71fc",
        "lesson_activity.key": "70cac4fc-e93e-4968-9dfa-1f4222424d13"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "798eb67c-211b-4eef-9c40-d47081054eb9",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Students use Python to write the same three normalization rules they followed in the Activity Guide.\n\n",
        "duration": "20",
        "name": "Normalizing the Catalog in Python"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "798eb67c-211b-4eef-9c40-d47081054eb9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "70cac4fc-e93e-4968-9dfa-1f4222424d13"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ec374d2a-d4f3-4170-9562-c87c66a54f64",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "In a moment, you’ll have a chance to try implementing these rules for yourself in Python. When you do, you might end up with a list of tokens that you want to join back together into a string.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ec374d2a-d4f3-4170-9562-c87c66a54f64",
        "lesson_activity.key": "70cac4fc-e93e-4968-9dfa-1f4222424d13"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1da91e55-dce8-478f-a036-ef597a6ec792",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce students to the `join` string method."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1da91e55-dce8-478f-a036-ef597a6ec792",
        "lesson_activity.key": "70cac4fc-e93e-4968-9dfa-1f4222424d13"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "383e1bf7-83ee-406b-ae1e-1ca398162894",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "`join` is a string method. It’s called on a string, like `\" \"`, and joins all of the elements of a list by placing that string between them. Notice in this example that the individual elements of the words list—\"the\", \"volcano\", and \"book\"—become joined together, with a space between them.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "383e1bf7-83ee-406b-ae1e-1ca398162894",
        "lesson_activity.key": "70cac4fc-e93e-4968-9dfa-1f4222424d13"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "73fd33af-0769-4566-bafc-f8bab727bbe8",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Normalize a Catalog"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "73fd33af-0769-4566-bafc-f8bab727bbe8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "70cac4fc-e93e-4968-9dfa-1f4222424d13"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e668d76d-c271-4a01-98ca-f1a62c9dbba8",
      "position": 14,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Check Your Understanding"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e668d76d-c271-4a01-98ca-f1a62c9dbba8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "70cac4fc-e93e-4968-9dfa-1f4222424d13"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "61c4a505-2c8c-43e3-8bb8-51904e5bdd2d",
      "position": 15,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 3, Levels 1–4.\n- Level 1: Students implement the lowercase rule.\n- Level 2: Students implement the rule that eliminates punctuation. They convert the given text into a list of word tokens.\n- Level 3: Students remove stop words, keeping only those word tokens that are not stop words.\n- Level 4: Students complete a Check Your Understanding.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Watch for these student challenges:\n- Students who call `.lower()` without assigning its result back to a variable. Ask: “Where does the lowercase text go?” The result has to be stored in a variable (e.g., `text = text.lower()`).\n- Students who type their regular expression without the `r` prefix or without quotes.\n- Students might finish early. Prompt early finishers to print each entry beside its normalized form (e.g., `print(entry, normalize(entry)`) inside the counting loop) and compare it with the Standard Form column on their guide.\n- If students struggle with additional logic or syntactic issues, encourage them to ask peers or to ask AI Tutor.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Uses Level 3 as an opportunity to assess students’ ability to manipulate text programmatically. By Level 3, students should have successfully implemented all three text normalization rules.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "61c4a505-2c8c-43e3-8bb8-51904e5bdd2d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "70cac4fc-e93e-4968-9dfa-1f4222424d13"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4ffe8521-6efa-4fc1-b357-4c7861125f22",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to answer the Question of the Day in their Unit Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4ffe8521-6efa-4fc1-b357-4c7861125f22",
        "lesson_activity.key": "084bd187-e337-4e48-95a6-2ba8e03cef1b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6f7a417a-6602-40ec-9d12-a6358e4ae8db",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *How do you think a search engine like Google finds the information you’re looking for?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Prompt students to share their intuitions about how searching for information online works. The primary goal is for students to share their ideas—as such, there are no right or wrong answers. Aim to guide the discussion towards a sense that search works by finding content similar to what a user is searching for. And to find out whether content is similar, it might be helpful to look at the words in that content."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6f7a417a-6602-40ec-9d12-a6358e4ae8db",
        "lesson_activity.key": "13129953-7963-4064-99d3-295f19100377"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d55d85c7-c91d-4706-a25d-c6a7675b895b",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "There many different algorithms behind the search engines you might use nearly every day. However, they all share a common goal: to show you information that’s similar to what you’re looking for. Today, we’ll learn about strategies for analyzing text that could be used to build a simple search tool.\n",
        "remarks": true,
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Consider defining the term \"search engine\" for students who may not be familiar with the term. A search engine can be considered to be a tool that lets users search to find information online.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d55d85c7-c91d-4706-a25d-c6a7675b895b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "13129953-7963-4064-99d3-295f19100377"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "dcd5afb2-9bcd-4cf4-8b38-5b7f7e02bb2e",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define _frequency analysis_.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "dcd5afb2-9bcd-4cf4-8b38-5b7f7e02bb2e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "13129953-7963-4064-99d3-295f19100377"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d28c70ff-0c73-4416-96e0-94be8e5a1438",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Frequency analysis is about breaking text into parts—such as words—and counting how often those parts appear in the text. With frequency analysis, you can quickly get a sense of what a document is about. That proves useful for tools like search engines.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d28c70ff-0c73-4416-96e0-94be8e5a1438",
        "lesson_activity.key": "13129953-7963-4064-99d3-295f19100377"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5e9964e5-2902-46d3-a4b3-c25ed8e0c063",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5e9964e5-2902-46d3-a4b3-c25ed8e0c063",
        "lesson_activity.key": "13129953-7963-4064-99d3-295f19100377"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f044201e-513e-4572-9543-c04ac6ea1bbb",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Students practice frequency analysis by hand and build their intuition for why frequently analysis is useful when searching for information.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the Frequency Analysis Activity Guide.",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Understanding Frequency Analysis"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f044201e-513e-4572-9543-c04ac6ea1bbb",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9f65c9ce-07c6-4f97-ae49-3fe1af2892d7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b23d6042-3504-4208-9f3a-3c901a7c381a",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "In this activity guide, your job is to help someone find the most relevant website based on what they searched for. First, you’ll count up how frequently three keywords appear in each of the websites you’re given. Then, you’ll make a recommendation about which website they should look at.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b23d6042-3504-4208-9f3a-3c901a7c381a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9f65c9ce-07c6-4f97-ae49-3fe1af2892d7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "74536571-3e2e-4923-bd29-7ff0e86a9702",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to complete the Activity Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work, look for those who aren’t sure how to perform the frequency analysis.\n- Prompt them to read each site’s title and text word by word, then tally each time they see the keyword. Suggest circling, underlining, or boxing each keyword as they read, so they can find and count the marked words afterward.\n- Remind students to count the title as well as the text. If a student’s “pizza” count for Site 2 is 1 instead of 2, they probably skipped the title.\n- When a student notices that the “pizza” search recommends the garlic bread recipe, encourage students to reflect on that conclusion. Help students think through why the top result doesn’t match their intuition, and help them brainstorm ways that the algorithm could be adjusted to produce better results.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What pages did your analysis recommend? What changes could you make to the scoring algorithm so that it produces better results?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Prompt students to reflect on the Activity Guide. Strong responses will recommend Page 1 for “tomato” and Page 3 for “pizza,” because what the user searched for appears most frequently in those sites. Many students will also point out that the “pizza” recommendation is clearly wrong, since Site 3 is a garlic bread recipe. Guide the discussion by helping students brainstorm approaches that would produce better results. For example, some students might point out that where a word appears matters too: a word that appears in a title is probably more important than a word that appears in the body of the page. Students might suggest algorithms that prioritize words in the title more as a way to produce better results.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "74536571-3e2e-4923-bd29-7ff0e86a9702",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9f65c9ce-07c6-4f97-ae49-3fe1af2892d7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "110a1139-fc7f-40bd-bc34-f4284b1cec54",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "As you’ve seen, frequency analysis can help a computer figure out what information someone is looking for. It’s a useful heuristic, though it’s not always guaranteed to produce the best results.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "110a1139-fc7f-40bd-bc34-f4284b1cec54",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9f65c9ce-07c6-4f97-ae49-3fe1af2892d7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e6552066-84c5-4515-a579-4ce90f5b8ccc",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Students implement frequency analysis in Python, completing a program that can recommend sites based on a search term.\n",
        "duration": "20",
        "name": "Frequency Analysis in Python"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e6552066-84c5-4515-a579-4ce90f5b8ccc",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9f65c9ce-07c6-4f97-ae49-3fe1af2892d7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c555132f-fc7b-4285-abd9-e734b782e4c0",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Let’s implement frequency analysis in Python. Your goal is to take a piece of text and create a dictionary. In that dictionary, words are keys. Their values are how often those words appear in the text.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c555132f-fc7b-4285-abd9-e734b782e4c0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9f65c9ce-07c6-4f97-ae49-3fe1af2892d7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5f5a593c-2fe1-466d-9e0e-569dc42af0b0",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Show students the pseudocode they’ll implement in Python in level 1.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5f5a593c-2fe1-466d-9e0e-569dc42af0b0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9f65c9ce-07c6-4f97-ae49-3fe1af2892d7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4ca642dd-7860-451c-84b0-e69313e1d8d1",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Here’s the function you’ll finish in just a moment. This function takes a variable, called text, as an argument—just like we saw on the previous slide. It returns frequencies, a dictionary where keys are words and their values are how often they appear in the given text.\n\nIn Level 2, you'll use this same function again. Each site has a title and content, so you'll count the words in both. Then you'll give the title count more weight and add it to the content count. This will help the program find the best site for a search.\n\nNow, you'll get a chance to practice using a Python dictionary to count words, and use those counts to build a simple search engine.\n\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4ca642dd-7860-451c-84b0-e69313e1d8d1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9f65c9ce-07c6-4f97-ae49-3fe1af2892d7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "278b85b6-0e42-4964-a68e-299d8e9ef78f",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Count Frequencies"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "278b85b6-0e42-4964-a68e-299d8e9ef78f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9f65c9ce-07c6-4f97-ae49-3fe1af2892d7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "87ed7406-44d7-4e76-949f-0b9c62201734",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Check Your Understanding"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "87ed7406-44d7-4e76-949f-0b9c62201734",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9f65c9ce-07c6-4f97-ae49-3fe1af2892d7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c8589ed2-3f1a-48df-a7f5-a28c8e26a066",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to work on Lesson 4, Levels 1-3.\n- Level 1: Students complete the `get_frequencies` function by building a dictionary of word frequencies. The program then uses those counts to search the recipe sites for a keyword and print the site where the keyword appears most often.\n- Level 2: Students weight the title of each site and add that value to the content count, so that a keyword in a title counts for more.\n- Level 3: Students complete a Check for Understanding.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students complete the programs, look for possible areas of confusion.\n- Students may not understand how to work with dictionaries to represent frequencies. Help them with the syntax for checking membership in a dictionary, adding a new value to a dictionary, and updating an existing value in a dictionary.\n- In Level 2, students might replace the content count instead of adding to it, or calculate only the title score. Remind them that a site's score is the weighted title count plus the content count.\n- In Level 2, students may get an error when the keyword isn't in a title or in the content. Help them check if keyword in the dictionary before looking up its count, and ask what they think a missing word should be worth.\n- Students may mix up `title_frequencies` and `content_frequencies`. Ask which dictionary holds which text.\n- When students test the program, point them to the keywords \"pizza,\" \"soup\" and \"taco.\" Ask them to notice how the best site changes from Level 1 to Level 2, and whether the result seems more helpful. That sets up the Check for Understanding."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c8589ed2-3f1a-48df-a7f5-a28c8e26a066",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9f65c9ce-07c6-4f97-ae49-3fe1af2892d7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "64648c9d-d664-458f-b536-2d37d70f933b",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to answer the Question of the Day in their Unit Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "64648c9d-d664-458f-b536-2d37d70f933b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "49fdd975-f1f2-4e59-9ed0-deb424eca4ce"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b1589669-a591-408a-b5d4-f66c7362faa6",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the scenario: a video website, a cooking channel with three new videos, and the transcript of each video."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b1589669-a591-408a-b5d4-f66c7362faa6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "aae47b6f-f1a6-4f7e-b420-119d563f3a0c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "37caad5a-db67-4ad7-952b-874b909345a3",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "In this lesson, we’ll help a video website. A cooking channel has just posted three videos: “Fluffy Pancakes”, “Tomato Soup” and “Garlic Bread”. The site has a transcript of each one, the words the cook said, and it needs a few words that say what each video is about: its keywords.",
        "remarks": true,
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "The channel and its videos are fictional, invented to convey the ideas in this lesson."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "37caad5a-db67-4ad7-952b-874b909345a3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "aae47b6f-f1a6-4f7e-b420-119d563f3a0c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ccf5c067-738a-4127-a717-50a08b77c202",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce keywords."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ccf5c067-738a-4127-a717-50a08b77c202",
        "lesson_activity.key": "aae47b6f-f1a6-4f7e-b420-119d563f3a0c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "705ad5ef-1c20-4bcf-8489-97d45628936d",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "A keyword is a word that says what a piece of text is about. When you search a video site, the site matches what you typed against each video’s keywords, so a video with the right keywords is the one you find. For the cooking channel, the site needs one or two keywords for each video.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "705ad5ef-1c20-4bcf-8489-97d45628936d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "aae47b6f-f1a6-4f7e-b420-119d563f3a0c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8d7fe9bf-7339-4239-a2a5-a34bab332ee4",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Prompt students to critique the site’s unhelpful keywords.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *The site’s first try picked each video’s most common word as its keyword. How helpful are these keywords? Why?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Prompt students to reflect on what makes a keyword helpful or unhelpful. Strong responses will identify that the keyword is the same for every video, so it tells a searcher nothing. In fact, “pan” is quite possibly the very worst keyword for a cooking channel: every video uses a pan. What matters is how the videos differ. Once this point is made, steer the discussion towards the kinds of words that might be more helpful. Particularly strong responses will identify that the words worth using are the ones one transcript uses but the others do not. Students might also frame this as the characteristics of good and bad keywords: a good keyword appears frequently in a single transcript, while a word that appears in many transcripts is probably a bad keyword.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the two traits of a good keyword: appearing frequently in a transcript and appearing in few other transcripts."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8d7fe9bf-7339-4239-a2a5-a34bab332ee4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "aae47b6f-f1a6-4f7e-b420-119d563f3a0c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "56ad0827-dc25-4bc7-a29c-04d8ecf45446",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "The keywords we just saw illustrate one of the limitations of frequency analysis. A word may be common in a transcript, but that does not mean it tells us what matters in that transcript. Instead, we should look for words that both appear frequently in a transcript and are relatively unique to it, appearing in few other transcripts.",
        "remarks": true,
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Consider clarifying that “document” is a more general term for a piece of text. In this scenario, a document is one video’s transcript."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "56ad0827-dc25-4bc7-a29c-04d8ecf45446",
        "lesson_activity.key": "aae47b6f-f1a6-4f7e-b420-119d563f3a0c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "51caa8a5-4f0b-4dbf-bb6d-ae7bce35065e",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "51caa8a5-4f0b-4dbf-bb6d-ae7bce35065e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "aae47b6f-f1a6-4f7e-b420-119d563f3a0c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "21ad4b7f-2b9c-4f75-a11d-cb884f8a0797",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Students compare three possible keywords for the “Fluffy Pancakes” video by computing term frequency, document frequency and inverse document frequency and, after the stop sign, tf-idf.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the Video Keywords Activity Guide. Students work individually; neighbors may compare counts.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Prompt students to read the three transcripts on page 1.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the premise of the Activity Guide.",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Introducing tf-idf"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "21ad4b7f-2b9c-4f75-a11d-cb884f8a0797",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7c077870-6b5f-470e-a8df-bbad801155f1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4221398a-f436-4abc-91fb-e9364e419203",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "In the Activity Guide, you’re comparing three possible keywords for the “Fluffy Pancakes” video: “pan”, “butter” and “batter”. Remember, you’re looking for words that both appear frequently in the transcript and appear in few other transcripts.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4221398a-f436-4abc-91fb-e9364e419203",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7c077870-6b5f-470e-a8df-bbad801155f1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "57d01ec2-4b76-462c-a4cb-ce20f79ab27a",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Prompt students to calculate term frequency, document frequency and inverse document frequency for each term, then stop at the stop sign."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "57d01ec2-4b76-462c-a4cb-ce20f79ab27a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7c077870-6b5f-470e-a8df-bbad801155f1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d6396457-65b2-4ba0-ac92-1b9835d8ded7",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "To find keywords that appear frequently in one transcript and in few others, computational linguists use three helpful statistics. You’ll calculate these in the Activity Guide. The first row of each table is a worked example, “flour”. Stop at the stop sign.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d6396457-65b2-4ba0-ac92-1b9835d8ded7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7c077870-6b5f-470e-a8df-bbad801155f1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "275cde29-2140-43bc-ab31-ada194b2d71a",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Watch for these student challenges:\n- Students might count “pancakes” or “pans” as “pan”. Remind them to count the exact word only; the program will match exact tokens the same way.\n- Students might write the document frequency as the number of times the term appears in all three transcripts. The document frequency is the number of transcripts that use the term at all, 1 to 3.\n- Students might divide the wrong way round. Point to the printed equation: 3 divided by the document frequency.\n- Students might copy the worked example’s numbers. The example shows the form of the work for “flour”; each term has its own numbers.\n- If students finish early, ask them to predict which of the three terms will score highest, and why.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Reveal the correct calculations for term frequency, document frequency and inverse document frequency. Then, briefly review the definitions of each statistic from the Activity Guide."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "275cde29-2140-43bc-ab31-ada194b2d71a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7c077870-6b5f-470e-a8df-bbad801155f1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c823c8a1-f6d3-4ac5-aa39-0bac99e0de72",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Term frequency is the number of times a term appears in one document. A document is any one piece of text. On the cooking channel, each transcript is a document. The term “pan” appears four times in the “Fluffy Pancakes” transcript.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c823c8a1-f6d3-4ac5-aa39-0bac99e0de72",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7c077870-6b5f-470e-a8df-bbad801155f1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "851147a5-ba2b-450a-b89d-c7d982c8c9db",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Document frequency is the number of transcripts that contain a term. And inverse document frequency is the total number of transcripts divided by the document frequency.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "851147a5-ba2b-450a-b89d-c7d982c8c9db",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7c077870-6b5f-470e-a8df-bbad801155f1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "877b61ed-3601-47dd-84a5-6742d5339806",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Point out that a higher document frequency results in a lower inverse document frequency, and vice versa."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "877b61ed-3601-47dd-84a5-6742d5339806",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7c077870-6b5f-470e-a8df-bbad801155f1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "aa08e605-e625-4a08-adc6-1fbf9ab6b220",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Do you notice that, when a term has a higher document frequency, it also has a lower inverse document frequency, and vice versa? For example, “pan” has a document frequency of 3 but an inverse document frequency of 1. The term “batter” has a document frequency of 1 but an inverse document frequency of 3.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "aa08e605-e625-4a08-adc6-1fbf9ab6b220",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7c077870-6b5f-470e-a8df-bbad801155f1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "578ed397-798c-4551-ba28-9161cec36b19",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce term frequency (TF) and inverse document frequency (IDF) as statistical measures of the two traits of good keywords introduced in the Warm Up."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "578ed397-798c-4551-ba28-9161cec36b19",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7c077870-6b5f-470e-a8df-bbad801155f1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0ed40d97-605c-43ca-91a6-be9ec93e8dfb",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Remember how we said a good keyword is one that appears frequently in a text while appearing in few other texts? Term frequency (TF) and inverse document frequency (IDF) give us a numeric way of talking about these two traits. A keyword that has both a high term frequency and a high inverse document frequency might, in fact, be a good keyword.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0ed40d97-605c-43ca-91a6-be9ec93e8dfb",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7c077870-6b5f-470e-a8df-bbad801155f1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "004e399f-d9f9-4246-a0da-b073b4ad1d68",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Which of the three terms would you choose as the keyword for the “Fluffy Pancakes” video? Why?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Prompt students to review the statistics they’ve calculated and decide which term might be the best keyword. Strong responses will make the case for “batter”, given it has a high term frequency and a high inverse document frequency. Students should see that “pan”, while it has the highest term frequency, has the lowest inverse document frequency, making it unlikely to be a strong keyword. And “butter”, while it has a higher inverse document frequency than “pan”, has a low term frequency. Aim to draw out an intuition that a term with both a high term frequency and a high inverse document frequency is likely a good keyword."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "004e399f-d9f9-4246-a0da-b073b4ad1d68",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7c077870-6b5f-470e-a8df-bbad801155f1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "50bab4a4-58cb-4636-ab66-22acf3d0810c",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "description": "It does seem like “batter” would be a good keyword, given that it has a high term frequency and a high inverse document frequency. As it turns out, there’s a way to formalize this intuition we’ve been discussing.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "50bab4a4-58cb-4636-ab66-22acf3d0810c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7c077870-6b5f-470e-a8df-bbad801155f1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "dfcf8e9f-aded-4236-9989-2da1bf423be0",
      "position": 14,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce students to tf-idf: a measure of how important a keyword is in a piece of text."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "dfcf8e9f-aded-4236-9989-2da1bf423be0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7c077870-6b5f-470e-a8df-bbad801155f1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "76e53979-dd09-49e3-a70f-dcdd1824281a",
      "position": 15,
      "properties": {
        "description": "One statistical measure, called tf-idf, is calculated by multiplying term frequency (TF) by inverse document frequency (IDF). The result is a score that increases as a keyword meets not one, but both, of the two traits of good keywords we discussed.",
        "remarks": true,
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "This version of tf-idf is a simplified version that still conveys the core idea. A more widely used measure multiplies TF by the log of IDF, which has the effect of better compressing scores."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "76e53979-dd09-49e3-a70f-dcdd1824281a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7c077870-6b5f-470e-a8df-bbad801155f1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a643d426-9e45-49c5-8a85-39e03fb503d2",
      "position": 16,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Give students two minutes to complete the second table of the Activity Guide: the term frequency, the inverse document frequency and their product for each term.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Watch for students who multiply the term frequency by the document frequency instead of the inverse document frequency. Point to the column headers: the second factor is the last column of the first table.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *tf-idf*."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a643d426-9e45-49c5-8a85-39e03fb503d2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7c077870-6b5f-470e-a8df-bbad801155f1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "de1ecc19-e7b0-4ab7-b5c2-3a0c91d45324",
      "position": 17,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Students complete a program that scores every word of every transcript: first the function that counts term frequency, then the function that computes inverse document frequency and the tf-idf score.",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "tf-idf in Python"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "de1ecc19-e7b0-4ab7-b5c2-3a0c91d45324",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7c077870-6b5f-470e-a8df-bbad801155f1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e9e59b28-7812-41aa-91ad-b77c6771d395",
      "position": 18,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Now you’ll do the same scoring in Python. The program reads the three transcripts and scores every word of each one; you’ll write the function that counts term frequency first, then inverse document frequency, and turn the score into tf-idf.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e9e59b28-7812-41aa-91ad-b77c6771d395",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7c077870-6b5f-470e-a8df-bbad801155f1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "775c0424-dfa9-45c6-bb19-52e24b6fa943",
      "position": 19,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Find the Keywords"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "775c0424-dfa9-45c6-bb19-52e24b6fa943",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7c077870-6b5f-470e-a8df-bbad801155f1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6f731d36-f235-4f5d-bb09-9a5d6a93685d",
      "position": 20,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Check Your Understanding"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6f731d36-f235-4f5d-bb09-9a5d6a93685d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7c077870-6b5f-470e-a8df-bbad801155f1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "291bc77f-cf4a-4fd3-9afc-b020682081f3",
      "position": 21,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 5, Levels 1 to 3.\n- **Level 1:** Students write the function that counts term frequency, and every video gets “pan” as its keyword.\n- **Level 2:** Students write inverse document frequency and update the score to tf-idf.\n- **Level 3:** Students complete a Check Your Understanding.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Watch for these student challenges:\n- Students might not be sure where the program starts. There is no main function: the code after the functions runs from top to bottom. The comments name each step.\n- Students might compare the term with the whole list instead of with each token. Prompt them to loop over the tokens and compare each one with the term.\n- Students might count how many times the term appears instead of how many videos use it. The loop is over videos. Each video counts once.\n- Students might update the score before their inverse document frequency returns a real value. The placeholder returns 1, so the scores look unchanged until TODO 2 is done.\n- Students might divide the wrong way round. The number of videos is divided by the count, as on the Activity Guide.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use Level 2 and Level 3 as assessment opportunities. Take note of whether students compute inverse document frequency and tf-idf correctly, and whether they can explain how a fourth video changes a score."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "291bc77f-cf4a-4fd3-9afc-b020682081f3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7c077870-6b5f-470e-a8df-bbad801155f1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3acdc9ee-0ecd-4df4-be5a-abb4502c4190",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to answer the Question of the Day in their Unit Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3acdc9ee-0ecd-4df4-be5a-abb4502c4190",
        "lesson_activity.key": "53bc8fb2-f7f6-4d4d-90cf-048f9102fed6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2f03b5d3-aaf4-4c69-8ea0-9ee06e662836",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *You receive this text message on your phone. Do you believe it? Why or why not?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students look at a text message and decide whether or not they would trust it. Strong responses will both identify that the message likely should not be believed and will also identify specific characteristics of the message that helped them reach that conclusion. For example, students might point out the offer of money, the use of all caps, the use of exclamation points, or the request for an immediate response as reasons why the message might be suspicious."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2f03b5d3-aaf4-4c69-8ea0-9ee06e662836",
        "lesson_activity.key": "57e8adae-4ed0-45e5-ac1f-ce7a4b61af04"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "84e05437-fea5-4713-870d-40d99cf33c8b",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "We know that AI can be used to classify data into different categories. One common use case for this is spam filtering: classifying a message as either spam or not spam. Today, we’ll use what we know about language processing to try to solve this problem.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "84e05437-fea5-4713-870d-40d99cf33c8b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "57e8adae-4ed0-45e5-ac1f-ce7a4b61af04"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5c262ca7-cf92-4aa3-a7d5-017d9ebb2658",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5c262ca7-cf92-4aa3-a7d5-017d9ebb2658",
        "lesson_activity.key": "57e8adae-4ed0-45e5-ac1f-ce7a4b61af04"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "aa4e08cf-0a39-407d-8a6d-dfa4c4a769ba",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Students consider what features are helpful for text classification. Students start by brainstorming features independently. Then, they consider small samples of data to guide their ideas. They also consider cases in which the features they identified might not work perfectly for classification.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Group students into groups of 2 to 3. Ask students to discuss for a minute or two what features they could use to identify spam messages. Encourage students to be precise: ideally, these features should be described in such a way that they could be encoded into a computer (“Does the message say ‘URGENT’ in capital letters?” is a more precise question than “Does the message sound suspicious?”, for example).\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Give students’ groups a few minutes to look at these examples of spam text messages. Students should look at the messages and use them to add to their list of features that might be helpful for identifying spam messages.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students look at the same text messages, help them find patterns in the sample messages. Some students might notice particular words (“free”, “claim”, and “urgent”, for example) which are more likely to appear in spam messages. Students might also notice the presence of words in all capital letters, the use of punctuation (like “!!”), the use of urgent language (“next 10 minutes”, “now”, “last chance”), or requests for sensitive or dangerous actions (sharing a password or clicking a link). If students struggle to identify specific qualities, start from first principles and ask students whether they would trust any of these messages and invite them to share why.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Once students have had a chance to add to their list, invite a few groups to share some of their features. Write down summaries of the features on the board so that everyone can see them—students will refer to these in the next part of the activity.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Can you think of examples where a message might match some of these features but not be spam? How should our classifier handle these cases?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** This discussion surfaces an important point: the features students have identified aren’t perfect for determining if a message is spam. If students identified all-caps words or exclamation points as indications of spam, students might mention that an excited friend might also text in all caps or with exclamation points. If students identify that words of urgency are indications of spam, students might also mention that a business that does urgently need to deliver a message might use similar language. The goal here is for students to recognize that each of these features is a clue, not a definitive answer. Students should then suggest ideas for how a classifier should handle this fact. There are multiple possibilities: students might say that we need more information about the message (What time was it sent? Did it come from a known contact? Has this number been in communication before?), students might suggest features that could identify a non-spam message (for example, the first person pronoun “I” might be more common in non-spam messages), and students might suggest that using multiple features together could help to reduce the likelihood of an error.",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Brainstorming Text Features"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "aa4e08cf-0a39-407d-8a6d-dfa4c4a769ba",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e84de98d-c9c1-406b-a628-a6eb6b9b5043"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3d911ecf-9930-419e-99d7-0ad52771092c",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "We’ve seen how classifiers can look at multiple features together to try to find rules that do a good job of classifying. In the next activity, you’ll have a chance to try building one for yourself in Python.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3d911ecf-9930-419e-99d7-0ad52771092c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e84de98d-c9c1-406b-a628-a6eb6b9b5043"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "27fd5b36-fea3-42f1-86bb-a760384bef77",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Students write a Python program to build a spam classifier using scikit-learn. Students start with implementing a specified list of features, then they try adding a feature of their own to see how it impacts the classifier.\n",
        "duration": "20",
        "name": "Building a Spam Classifier"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "27fd5b36-fea3-42f1-86bb-a760384bef77",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e84de98d-c9c1-406b-a628-a6eb6b9b5043"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "91a588fd-13fb-4f3d-b24a-3021302e9427",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "It would be hard for us to write all the rules for when something is spam or not. Instead, we can give text features to a decision tree classifier and let the classifier figure out how best to predict whether a message is spam.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "91a588fd-13fb-4f3d-b24a-3021302e9427",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e84de98d-c9c1-406b-a628-a6eb6b9b5043"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "361e1fec-d007-46b8-9e7e-8415b602ee51",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Remind students about the goal of the classifier. The classifier will take a text message as input and produce as output a prediction (“Spam” or “Not Spam”). The examples here demonstrate why this might be tricky: the presence of all-caps letters and exclamation points might be indicators of spam messages, but they won’t work all the time.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce students to and remind students of some helpful Python syntax and functions for working with strings.\n- Students can loop over a string to access each character one at a time.\n- The `len` function returns the number of elements in a sequence, like the number of characters in a string.\n- The `count` function returns the number of times a substring appears inside a string.\n- The `split` function splits a string wherever there are spaces and returns a list of strings.\n- The `isupper` function checks if the letters in a string are all uppercase, returning `True` or `False`.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "When students begin working in Python lab in the next level, it might be helpful to leave this slide visible to remind students of syntax they can use to work with strings.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "361e1fec-d007-46b8-9e7e-8415b602ee51",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e84de98d-c9c1-406b-a628-a6eb6b9b5043"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "559383f7-6bb6-4cce-a132-00612a1b270c",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Classify Spam"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "559383f7-6bb6-4cce-a132-00612a1b270c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e84de98d-c9c1-406b-a628-a6eb6b9b5043"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3b33282c-f868-45db-9733-6f009a659d80",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Check Your Understanding"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3b33282c-f868-45db-9733-6f009a659d80",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e84de98d-c9c1-406b-a628-a6eb6b9b5043"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "fe4475c2-e475-4e0c-a607-82f33ee9ece2",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Levels 1-3.\n- Level 1: Students write a function to extract three text features from a message, so that those features can be used in a decision tree classifier.\n- Level 2: Students implement one or more features of their own to try to improve the accuracy of their classifier.\n- Level 3: Students complete a Check Your Understanding.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work through the levels, help them through common areas where they might get stuck.\n- On Level 1, students are most likely to get stuck with the string manipulation part of the exercise: the level expects students to iterate over strings or use string methods to find useful information from those strings. The level provides AI Tutor to help students if they get stuck, but you might also want to show and remind students of the Python string manipulation functions found on the previous slide. You can also help them think through what operations they might want to perform on strings to get at the information they’re looking for: they might need to iterate over strings one character at a time, split a string into words, or check the length and characters within a string.\n- For splitting a string into words, it’s okay if students don’t consider more sophisticated forms of tokenization: splitting based on spaces is okay. That said, if students would like to try more sophisticated forms of tokenization, they’re welcome to.\n- If students are really struggling with Level 1, help them with the syntax for the exclamation counting code and then invite them to try the dollar sign counting code on their own: the logic is very similar.\n- For the all-caps check, the example solution includes a check to verify that the word is more than one character long. This is to prevent words like “A” or “I” counting as all-caps words. If students leave out this check, their code will still work, but the accuracy may not be as high.\n- Students might see different accuracy values each time they run the program. This is to be expected, since the data is split randomly each time. Students might want to run their program multiple times to get a general sense for accuracy overall.\n- In Level 2, encourage students to take one of the ideas they brainstormed for spam features and implement it in code. There’s no one right answer, and it’s not expected that all students will necessarily improve the accuracy of their model: in some cases, the accuracy might stay the same or even decrease.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use Level 3 as an assessment opportunity to check whether students understand how the features they’re extracting are used in a classifier. Students should recognize whether their feature resulted in an increase, decrease, or no/minimal change in the accuracy of the classifier. Then, they should offer a suggestion as to why: a feature that significantly improved accuracy was probably adding useful information to the classifier that did a good job of splitting data into two groups. If a feature didn’t improve accuracy, it might not have done a good job of splitting this particular data into two groups, or not added additional information that wasn’t already provided by the other features.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "fe4475c2-e475-4e0c-a607-82f33ee9ece2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e84de98d-c9c1-406b-a628-a6eb6b9b5043"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "139807a2-f381-4131-aadb-1db3ddb22d87",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to answer the Question of the Day in their Unit Guide."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "139807a2-f381-4131-aadb-1db3ddb22d87",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4ef15ead-657d-4421-972c-ae25c54232d2"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7549c6c4-2ca4-4627-9667-8db3b7e1891f",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *The following are some of the words from a piece of text. What do you think the original text was about?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students try to solve the puzzle of what the original text represents, when presented with words from the text that aren’t in order. The correct answer is that the words are from a recipe for baking chocolate chip cookies. Students should identify that the presence of ingredients (vanilla, chocolate, butter, etc.) indicate that the text might be a recipe, and students who pay attention to the particular ingredients and verbs might identify the recipe as chocolate chip cookies. The goal is for students to realize that information can be contained in the words in text, even if those words aren’t in order.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7549c6c4-2ca4-4627-9667-8db3b7e1891f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "11406795-a7b6-4d65-bf06-61eb4a3687b2"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "57f079d1-1706-4e71-b1bf-bd6fc6aa6177",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Even though the words weren’t in the right order, we can still look at the words that make up text and learn information about the meaning of the original text. Today, we’ll see how computers can do that too.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "57f079d1-1706-4e71-b1bf-bd6fc6aa6177",
        "lesson_activity.key": "11406795-a7b6-4d65-bf06-61eb4a3687b2"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2ff0b0d6-4607-4078-9ed7-eb0043213863",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2ff0b0d6-4607-4078-9ed7-eb0043213863",
        "lesson_activity.key": "11406795-a7b6-4d65-bf06-61eb4a3687b2"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "55c54a9d-655d-453d-aef7-691c970359e8",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Students are introduced to bag of words as an approach for modeling language. Students look at how text can be represented using the bag of words model, then look at some examples of data represented with the bag of words model and practice interpreting what the data might mean.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define _bag of words_.",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Interpreting Bag of Words Models"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "55c54a9d-655d-453d-aef7-691c970359e8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "758ade4c-fbfd-4a96-a187-3bfd937e55b5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "750315dc-266e-4acb-b9d4-e5365ce56c7c",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "We’ve seen before that, to convert data into a form that’s easy for computers to work with, we need to turn it into numbers. One simple way to do that is with the bag of words model. The bag of words model takes text and represents it by counting which words appear and how many times they appear. It doesn’t care about sentence structure or anything else about the order that words are in.\n",
        "remarks": true,
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students might wonder about the description of bag of words as a “model”. Bag of words is considered a “model” in the sense that it is a way of representing or describing text data. This usage of the word “model” differs from its use in a term like “machine learning model,” where a machine learning model is specifically a system that uses data to make predictions. A bag of words model is a way to represent text data, but it does not by itself make predictions.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "750315dc-266e-4acb-b9d4-e5365ce56c7c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "758ade4c-fbfd-4a96-a187-3bfd937e55b5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "11adb05a-1c68-4d5f-85db-f39c4a6c1bdb",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Demonstrate an example of how the bag of words model might represent a piece of text. Show the example text and show its bag-of-words representation as a Python dictionary: the dictionary uses words as keys and the counts for each word as values. For example, since the word “be” appears twice in the original text, the key “be” is mapped to the value 2 in the dictionary. Also point out that the order of the dictionary doesn’t need to match the order of words in the original text.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Show students the text represented as a bag of words model. Tell students that the data comes from a recipe, but they aren’t told whether it’s a cookie recipe or a cake recipe.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Give students a chance to look at the data, then take a poll to see whether students believe it’s a cookie or cake recipe.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What kind of recipe is this? How do you know?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students look at text represented with the bag of words model and make a classification prediction based on the data. In this example, strong responses will pay attention to the specific words that are included in the data and consider whether those words offer an indication for what kind of recipe the text is. Students might notice that some words aren’t useful for determining subject matter (“the”, “you”, “it”), some words suggest a recipe but don’t help much in telling apart cookies and cake (“baking”, “before”), while other words are clearer indications that the recipe is about cookies (“dough”, “scoop”).\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Reveal the original text, showing that it was a cookie recipe."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "11adb05a-1c68-4d5f-85db-f39c4a6c1bdb",
        "lesson_activity.key": "dec5360c-b50f-4e77-a57b-064aabdcede5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "bc619038-a64d-4fe6-9396-c6b8d975e69d",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "The words present in the bag of words model gave us clues that helped us determine whether this was a cookie or cake recipe. “Scoop” and “chill”, for example, are much more likely to appear in a cookie recipe than a cake recipe.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "bc619038-a64d-4fe6-9396-c6b8d975e69d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "dec5360c-b50f-4e77-a57b-064aabdcede5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8e0f752d-0f16-4210-85d3-1d45ba20d4ff",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Show students the text represented as a bag of words model. Tell students that the data comes from a paleontologist’s description of a dinosaur, but the students aren’t told whether the dinosaur is an herbivore or a carnivore.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Give students a chance to look at the data, then take a poll to see whether students believe the description is of an herbivore or a carnivore.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What kind of dinosaur is this? How do you know?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students look at text represented with the bag of words model and make a classification prediction based on the data. In this example, it’s important for students to pay attention not only to which words are present in the data but also how frequently each word occurs. If students struggle to know where to begin, ask students to imagine how they would describe the teeth of an herbivore compared to a carnivore: teeth are likely to be relevant given that the word appears three times in the text. Help students consider that dinosaurs that are herbivores will tend to have flat teeth, whereas dinosaurs that are carnivores will tend to have sharp teeth. In this case, both “sharp” and “flat” appear in the dataset, but strong responses will notice that “sharp” appears twice whereas “flat” only appears once. While students might not know for sure, students might reasonably guess that the dinosaur is more likely to be a carnivore than an herbivore.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Reveal the original text, showing that it was a carnivore."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8e0f752d-0f16-4210-85d3-1d45ba20d4ff",
        "lesson_activity.key": "dec5360c-b50f-4e77-a57b-064aabdcede5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "038797e8-4d78-4dcf-a374-3e6e1d2e6cbc",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "The frequency of the words present in the bag of words model gave us clues that helped us determine whether this was an herbivore or a carnivore. “Sharp” and “teeth” appear frequently, which suggests that the dinosaur might be a carnivore.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "038797e8-4d78-4dcf-a374-3e6e1d2e6cbc",
        "lesson_activity.key": "dec5360c-b50f-4e77-a57b-064aabdcede5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "bdd34906-583e-4211-86cd-40355c68092c",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Show students the text represented as a bag of words model. Tell students that the data comes from a book review, but the students aren’t told whether the review is positive or negative.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Give students a chance to look at the data, then take a poll to see whether students believe the description is a positive review or a negative review.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What kind of book review is this? How do you know?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students look at text represented with the bag of words model and make a classification prediction based on the data. In this example, strong responses will identify that the bag of words model alone isn’t always enough to draw conclusions about the meaning of text. Some students might notice the presence of “good” and think the review might be positive; some students might notice the presence of “bad” and think the review might be negative; other students will notice that both appear with equal frequency and not be sure. The reality is that there isn’t a clearly correct answer: acknowledge and affirm students’ uncertainty when it is revealed during the discussion.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Show one possible sentence that corresponds to the bag of words data. In this case, the sentence suggests that the review is positive.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Show another possible sentence that corresponds to the bag of words data. In this case, the sentence suggests that the review is negative. Point out to students that the exact same bag of words data can represent sentences with entirely different meanings.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "bdd34906-583e-4211-86cd-40355c68092c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "dec5360c-b50f-4e77-a57b-064aabdcede5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6ca55e7a-41a4-481e-b107-1014455d81c6",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "The bag of words model is a simplification: it doesn’t always give us enough information to know exactly what text means. But it’s good enough to solve problems in many cases.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6ca55e7a-41a4-481e-b107-1014455d81c6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "dec5360c-b50f-4e77-a57b-064aabdcede5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "26e85ddc-0ddf-4e84-b73e-a32af6f9da35",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Students write code that computes bag of words features to build a classifier.\n",
        "duration": "20",
        "name": "Implementing Bag of Words"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "26e85ddc-0ddf-4e84-b73e-a32af6f9da35",
        "lesson_activity.key": "dec5360c-b50f-4e77-a57b-064aabdcede5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "be4144fa-542a-4475-8152-03afd886f116",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Now, you’ll have a chance to use the bag of words model to prepare data for a classifier that works with text.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "be4144fa-542a-4475-8152-03afd886f116",
        "lesson_activity.key": "dec5360c-b50f-4e77-a57b-064aabdcede5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "86e4d3f6-f538-45d2-b7f6-9422707b96da",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Implement Bag of Words"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "86e4d3f6-f538-45d2-b7f6-9422707b96da",
        "lesson_activity.key": "dec5360c-b50f-4e77-a57b-064aabdcede5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c427ad4d-49b8-4579-a8d0-ad64910a20f6",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Check Your Understanding"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c427ad4d-49b8-4579-a8d0-ad64910a20f6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "dec5360c-b50f-4e77-a57b-064aabdcede5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "dfa1c389-6f1b-4651-9cc1-b329b6ed3f4b",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Levels 1-2.\n- Level 1: Students write the logic to compute features for a decision tree classifier that works with text.\n- Level 2: Students complete a Check Your Understanding to evaluate how well they understand the capabilities and limits of a bag of words model.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work through the levels, help them through common areas where they might get stuck.\n- First, help students understand the structure of the function they are asked to implement. Students might not be sure what the expected output of the function should be: walk through how a dictionary mapping tokens to their frequency counts might look. It might also be helpful to refer students back to the previous activity, where they looked at Python dictionaries that match the expected format, and to Lesson 4 of the unit, where students wrote code to analyze the frequency of words.\n- Remind students that they can use the `tokenize` function to split their text into a list of tokens.\n- Students might not know how to implement the counting logic. Here, you can refer students to AI Tutor or give students suggestions for where to get started: it’s likely helpful for students to maintain a dictionary that they update over time. Remind students how to check whether a key is already in a dictionary (with the `in` keyword) and how to update the value stored in a dictionary.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Some students might read and ask about the rest of the starter code that they don’t write. Students don’t need to understand it to complete the levels, but the code is intentionally visible so that curious students can explore what the rest of the code is doing. If students ask, you can point out that the rest of the code takes students bag-of-words features and calculates a list of all words used across all pieces of input: this forms the “vocabulary” the model will learn. Then, the code uses each vocabulary word as a feature: in cases where a word doesn’t appear at all in the text, its value is set to 0. Then, the resulting data is used in a decision tree classifier."
          },
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use Level 2 as an opportunity to see whether students understand the capabilities and limits of a bag of words model. Strong responses will identify sentences where the meaning isn’t fully captured by the word frequencies alone, and something about the order of the words is relevant for meaning. If students have a hard time here, you might revisit the third example from the previous activity, where the word frequencies were insufficient for determining meaning.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "dfa1c389-6f1b-4651-9cc1-b329b6ed3f4b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "dec5360c-b50f-4e77-a57b-064aabdcede5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f237bfc1-7d97-4572-9296-e2b638dd157a",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to answer the Question of the Day in their Unit Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f237bfc1-7d97-4572-9296-e2b638dd157a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "79b8dfc2-6531-404d-9cb8-007a577fa610"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4e01e5b5-8882-4430-9f42-c32600feae27",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *It’s your birthday, and you just received one text message from a number you don’t have saved. Is it more likely from a teenage friend or a grandparent? Why?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Spend no more than a minute taking ideas from students and discussing them. There isn’t a single correct answer, but strong responses will likely identify that “Hi!” is generic enough of a message that it could have come from either a teenage friend or a grandparent. Some students might give answers justified by information particular to their scenario (their grandparents don’t text, their friends don’t usually capitalize “Hi!”, etc.). Some students might also make a mathematical argument: they have more teenage friends than they have grandparents, so absent any additional information, the message might be more likely from a friend. Other students might say they don’t know for sure. Help students see that while they might have a guess, the one word isn’t a lot of information from which to make a conclusion.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *It’s your birthday, and you just received two text messages from a number you don’t have saved. Is it more likely from a teenage friend or a grandparent? Why?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Spend no more than a minute taking ideas from students and discussing them. Students now have more data from which to try to answer the question. Again, answers will vary: strong responses might make a guess and justify it with evidence, or say they still don’t have enough information to know. Students might make arguments about whether their friends or their grandparents are more likely to use the cake emoji, more likely to separate texts into multiple messages, or more likely to spell out “Happy birthday” in full. Help students see that with more information, students might feel more confident in their answers, but still might not know.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *It’s your birthday, and you just received three text messages from a number you don’t have saved. Is it more likely from a teenage friend or a grandparent? Why?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Spend no more than a minute taking ideas from students and discussing them. Students are likely to conclude at this point that the messages are likely from a teenage friend: the use of abbreviations and emoji is likely more consistent with a teenage friend than a grandparent. Help students see both that it’s not impossible for the message to be from a grandparent (some grandparents might use abbreviations and emojis) and that statistically the amount of data we have might help us feel more confident that the message is from a teenage friend."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4e01e5b5-8882-4430-9f42-c32600feae27",
        "lesson_activity.key": "496bfb90-faf1-45a4-9fd3-fcb5df8a7971"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ab977bf2-9f96-409d-86f8-c634809694c2",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "When we’re not sure about something, more information can help us revise our estimates of how likely something is. That’s true for classifiers too. The more information we give them, the better they can calculate accurate probabilities for what category data belongs to.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ab977bf2-9f96-409d-86f8-c634809694c2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "496bfb90-faf1-45a4-9fd3-fcb5df8a7971"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "bd7f3109-d518-4207-89b9-6adc292bbe70",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "bd7f3109-d518-4207-89b9-6adc292bbe70",
        "lesson_activity.key": "496bfb90-faf1-45a4-9fd3-fcb5df8a7971"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2b5031a5-d334-4e2b-bb1b-038e634c180c",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Students are introduced to conditional probability as a tool for measuring the likelihood of something given some existing data. Students use conditional probability as a tool to evaluate the likelihood that a product review is positive or negative.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Remind students about sentiment analysis. Sentiment analysis was first introduced as a problem to solve in Unit 1. In this lesson, students will revisit sentiment analysis and look at algorithms that can learn what counts as positive or negative text.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Show some examples of product reviews. Point out that some of the reviews are positive (indicated by a smiling emoji) and some of the reviews are negative (indicated by a frowning emoji).",
        "duration": "20",
        "name": "Analyzing Product Reviews"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2b5031a5-d334-4e2b-bb1b-038e634c180c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a8d66aec-51d4-42cb-90bd-b95d56a41748"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7dcc46d2-c7fd-480d-ba3b-22e37d4a43d7",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Here’s some data we could use to train a classifier. The classifier gets some reviews that are positive and some reviews that are negative. The problem we’d like the classifier to solve is to take a new review and predict whether the review is positive or negative.\n",
        "remarks": true,
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "This lesson also includes a [supplementary Naive Bayes video](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=xxkpgxg1i5I) that you can use in place of this section of the slides or as an additional resource for students.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7dcc46d2-c7fd-480d-ba3b-22e37d4a43d7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a8d66aec-51d4-42cb-90bd-b95d56a41748"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "05d7278b-ea06-4876-a85e-7945207ee001",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Remind students about probability. Students were first introduced to probability in Unit 2.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "05d7278b-ea06-4876-a85e-7945207ee001",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a8d66aec-51d4-42cb-90bd-b95d56a41748"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b00ed38d-68b1-4ae2-b0b9-65577ebda7ba",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Classifiers can use probability to express how confident they are in a decision. A classifier might predict that a review is positive. But it’s often more helpful to us for the classifier to tell us how likely it is that the review is positive: if a classifier is 99% sure that a review is positive, that means something different than if a classifier is 51% sure that a review is positive.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b00ed38d-68b1-4ae2-b0b9-65577ebda7ba",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a8d66aec-51d4-42cb-90bd-b95d56a41748"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9ecda61e-c19a-4632-a8e9-babf3d523576",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Show students the notation for probability. Probability is typically written with a capital P and the name of the event being measured (in this case, A) in parentheses.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9ecda61e-c19a-4632-a8e9-babf3d523576",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a8d66aec-51d4-42cb-90bd-b95d56a41748"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "39820210-6a52-4505-bdb1-16079177488e",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Let’s try to answer this question based only on the five reviews of data we have in front of us.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "39820210-6a52-4505-bdb1-16079177488e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a8d66aec-51d4-42cb-90bd-b95d56a41748"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "cb8a6209-82f5-4faf-b2d5-a5d87227fc3e",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What is P(positive) — the probability that a review is positive?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students review what probability means and how to compute it. Help students see that the probability of a positive review, given the data, is 0.6: there are 3 positive reviews and 5 total reviews, so the probability is 3 ÷ 5 = 0.6. If students struggle here, remind them that probability is always between 0 (impossible) and 1 (certain), and they can compute probability among equally likely outcomes by taking the number of outcomes they care about (3 positive reviews) and dividing by the number of total outcomes (5 total reviews)."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "cb8a6209-82f5-4faf-b2d5-a5d87227fc3e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a8d66aec-51d4-42cb-90bd-b95d56a41748"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9b81adf6-9526-4f4b-a225-a612b8e7b86d",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "To make a better prediction as to whether a review is positive or negative, we need more information.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9b81adf6-9526-4f4b-a225-a612b8e7b86d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a8d66aec-51d4-42cb-90bd-b95d56a41748"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "30d32005-fca2-4e43-a6d6-758192c32f53",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define _conditional probability_.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "30d32005-fca2-4e43-a6d6-758192c32f53",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a8d66aec-51d4-42cb-90bd-b95d56a41748"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "75479718-291e-418e-9be4-d14b595cd9a6",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Conditional probability lets us calculate probabilities based on information we already know. For example, if we already know that certain words are in a product review, that can change the probability that the review is positive or negative.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "75479718-291e-418e-9be4-d14b595cd9a6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a8d66aec-51d4-42cb-90bd-b95d56a41748"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8a421cb2-0005-4824-8096-eaec7fcd5f79",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Show students the notation for probability. Conditional probability is written with the event that we’re measuring (in this case, A), followed by a vertical bar, followed by the event that we already know (in this case, B).\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students might find it helpful to think of the | symbol (the “given” symbol) as analogous to the divided-by symbol in math. To determine P(A | B) when considering equally likely outcomes, students can take the number of outcomes where A and B both happen and divide by the number of outcomes where B happens.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8a421cb2-0005-4824-8096-eaec7fcd5f79",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a8d66aec-51d4-42cb-90bd-b95d56a41748"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "db151e3b-30fb-4b6f-81a6-1a855c66eb47",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What is P(positive|\"love\") — the probability that a review is positive, given that the word “love” is in the review?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Help students calculate conditional probability. For some students, the answer might come intuitively, but for other students, they might need some guidance to understand the intuition. Strong responses will identify that the conditional probability is 0.67: there are 3 total reviews that have the word “love”, of which 2 are positive. So the conditional probability is 2 ÷ 3 = 0.67. If students struggle to reach that conclusion, help them walk through the process: point out that since we know the word “love” is in the review, we can ignore the data that doesn’t include the word “love”. That leaves us with 3 reviews, of which 2 are positive.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Group students into pairs."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "db151e3b-30fb-4b6f-81a6-1a855c66eb47",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a8d66aec-51d4-42cb-90bd-b95d56a41748"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "05734749-13e7-4b12-9771-f7864973830d",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Now you’ll have a chance to practice calculating conditional probability and use those results to make predictions.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "05734749-13e7-4b12-9771-f7864973830d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a8d66aec-51d4-42cb-90bd-b95d56a41748"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c80f1ec5-aa8c-417e-a087-6e0b23fde300",
      "position": 14,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Distribute a copy of the Product Review Activity Guide to each pair of students.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to complete the Product Reviews Activity Guide in pairs.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work through the activity guide, keep an eye out for the following:\n- Students who aren’t sure how to calculate conditional probability. Help students follow the instructions laid out in the activity guide: to compute the conditional probability that a review is positive given that a particular word is in the review, students take the number of positive reviews that have the word and divide by the total number of reviews that contain the word. Importantly, students can ignore any reviews that don’t contain the word when performing this calculation.\n- Some students might not know how to use their conditional probabilities to make a prediction. Help students look at and interpret the probability values they calculated, but also encourage them to use their intuition: for example, the word “amazing” sounds positive, and every review in the dataset that contains the word “amazing” is labeled as a positive review. That should be a strong indication to students that the review is likely positive, even though the word “cheap” sometimes appears in negative reviews.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If time permits at the end of the activity, have students discuss the question in the Making Predictions section, where students predict whether a review is positive or negative based on words that are present in the review. Students should identify that the word “amazing” is a very strong indicator of a positive review and that the word “cheap” is an indicator of a negative review, but not nearly as strong (there are positive reviews that include the word “cheap”). As a result, while there isn’t a single correct answer, a reasonable guess is that the review is positive.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c80f1ec5-aa8c-417e-a087-6e0b23fde300",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a8d66aec-51d4-42cb-90bd-b95d56a41748"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "dec0dc0c-ab6b-4bcd-8b03-ec1cfcb8e077",
      "position": 15,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Students implement a Naive Bayes classifier in Python to make predictions using conditional probability. Then, they experiment with their Naive Bayes classifier and analyze the classifier’s output.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define _Naive Bayes_.",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Implementing Naive Bayes"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "dec0dc0c-ab6b-4bcd-8b03-ec1cfcb8e077",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a8d66aec-51d4-42cb-90bd-b95d56a41748"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4541df08-bb00-4ebe-90ad-afeaf937e830",
      "position": 16,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Naive Bayes classifiers use the ideas we’ve been learning about in the past two lessons. They treat text like a bag of words and they make predictions using conditional probability: when given text, they calculate the probability that text belongs to a particular category by calculating conditional probabilities for all of the words in the bag of words.\n",
        "remarks": true,
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students might wonder where the name “Naive Bayes” comes from. “Bayes” comes from Thomas Bayes, a statistician who invented the key statistical ideas used in the algorithm. The “naive” part comes from the fact that the classifier makes some simplifying assumptions about probabilities to make the math easier: the result is an algorithm that is simpler to understand, but at the cost of some accuracy.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4541df08-bb00-4ebe-90ad-afeaf937e830",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a8d66aec-51d4-42cb-90bd-b95d56a41748"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4e86342e-1c35-4f60-9670-9fc3b6ee0b48",
      "position": 17,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Now, you’ll have a chance to implement and use a Naive Bayes classifier in Python.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4e86342e-1c35-4f60-9670-9fc3b6ee0b48",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a8d66aec-51d4-42cb-90bd-b95d56a41748"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "627bf2e3-6ab9-41ca-8cc1-9b3ee25b04ca",
      "position": 18,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Classify with Naive Bayes"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "627bf2e3-6ab9-41ca-8cc1-9b3ee25b04ca",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a8d66aec-51d4-42cb-90bd-b95d56a41748"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e3f2a63c-9ddc-414d-aadf-bb81471d4170",
      "position": 19,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Check Your Understanding"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e3f2a63c-9ddc-414d-aadf-bb81471d4170",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a8d66aec-51d4-42cb-90bd-b95d56a41748"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5404ed32-92bf-419e-9763-11a2af665d3f",
      "position": 20,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Levels 1-3.\n- Level 1: Students update a classification program that uses a decision tree classifier to use Naive Bayes instead.\n- Level 2: Students experiment with their Naive Bayes classifier and try different kinds of reviews to see how the classifier responds.\n- Level 3: Students complete a Check Your Understanding based on the reviews they wrote in the previous level.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work through the levels, help them through common areas where they might get stuck.\n- First, in Level 1, students might not be sure how to convert the decision tree classifier to a Naive Bayes classifier. The level introduces students to the only change they need to make: they just need to replace the name of the model type used when creating the model. Help students see that one nice advantage of using a library for machine learning is that it’s easy to swap out different models for each other to see how they behave.\n- In Level 2, students might get stuck with getting the model to produce particular responses. Encourage students to try simple inputs first: what happens if they just plug in a single word, or two words? Have students try words that they think lean positive or negative, or encourage students to look at the dataset to see for themselves what kinds of words the model was trained on.\n- Students might struggle to identify a case where a model is unsure or wrong. The model will often be unsure in cases where some words lean positive and some words lean negative: encourage students to try a mix of different kinds of words in their review. The model will be wrong in cases where the words lean in a direction that differs from the actual intent of the review. You might ask students to try writing a review that uses a very positive word like “amazing” but that is actually a negative review overall.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use Level 3 as an opportunity to evaluate whether students understand what a Naive Bayes classifier is doing. Students should be able to identify that a Naive Bayes classifier learns about particular words and whether they tend to be positive or negative. Using that information, students could come up with reviews that cause a Naive Bayes classifier to be unsure (because the review contains a mix of positive and negative words) or that cause a Naive Bayes classifier to be wrong (because the review contains words that lean towards one classification but the review actually has opposite sentiment). If students have a hard time here, you can refer back to the five example reviews earlier in the slides to see how some words like “love” can appear even in negative reviews.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5404ed32-92bf-419e-9763-11a2af665d3f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a8d66aec-51d4-42cb-90bd-b95d56a41748"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "eb0673c4-67c0-46f9-9851-dbc1d42cbf3f",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to answer the Question of the Day in their Unit Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "eb0673c4-67c0-46f9-9851-dbc1d42cbf3f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2836e58a-6574-4824-b459-637eab187071"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6dff9656-7c02-451c-9718-cbd5f3eaaded",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Motivate, via discussion, why students should learn about n-grams.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What can you tell about this story, based on the frequency of these tokens?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Prompt students to consider what story the tokens displayed might represent. Typical responses might suggest the story is about an owl and a mouse. Make a mental note of responses that may extrapolate too far, identifying meaning the given tokens don’t necessarily support. For example, a student might suggest it’s a story about an \"owl watching a mouse at night,\" because this is one possible combination of the frequent tokens. Hear a few different students’ suggestions, then continue on to the next discussion.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What can you tell about the story when you consider pairs of tokens instead?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Prompt students to (re-)consider what story the tokens displayed might represent. Strong responses will suggest what we can now be more sure of: that this is a story in which a mouse frequently watches an owl. Use this opportunity to address prior interpretations of the story that went too far. Point out that, while individual tokens could tell us the main characters or key actions in the story, it’s the order of those tokens that better illustrates what the story is about."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6dff9656-7c02-451c-9718-cbd5f3eaaded",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ec843f28-5731-4f85-a779-b9cdef6f9d99"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "21877c1a-68cf-4534-b0e9-952fe78dece1",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "While individual tokens can tell us a few things about a text like this story, they can’t tell us everything. Meaning in language comes not just from individual tokens, but from the order in which those tokens appear. That’s why, today, we’ll study a technique for considering the order of tokens in a text. The structure you’ll learn about today is the basis of many natural language processing systems.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "21877c1a-68cf-4534-b0e9-952fe78dece1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ec843f28-5731-4f85-a779-b9cdef6f9d99"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "62160c12-6c86-4b58-93e2-a859caf62531",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *n-gram*."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "62160c12-6c86-4b58-93e2-a859caf62531",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ec843f28-5731-4f85-a779-b9cdef6f9d99"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d806d477-a600-43d1-a107-3f3e9f5435e3",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "An n-gram is a sequence of N tokens in a row, where N is just a number we choose. When N is 1, we look at one token at a time, called a unigram. When N is 2, we look at sequences of two tokens, called bigrams. When N is 3, we look at sequences of three tokens, called trigrams. We can keep going: N of 4 gives us 4-grams, N of 5 gives us 5-grams, and so on.",
        "remarks": true,
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "As a pronunciation tip, put the stress on the first part of the word, saying \"BI-gram\" and \"TRI-gram\"."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d806d477-a600-43d1-a107-3f3e9f5435e3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ec843f28-5731-4f85-a779-b9cdef6f9d99"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "bb2f0985-86b4-443a-af97-9713ef6d1acd",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "bb2f0985-86b4-443a-af97-9713ef6d1acd",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ec843f28-5731-4f85-a779-b9cdef6f9d99"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "41221ff0-d07b-4bd0-80a4-e1b4f3baea3f",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Invite students to work together to understand and formalize the process of finding bigrams in a piece of text.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Prompt students to find a partner, then pull out a blank sheet of paper and a pen or a pencil.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Prompt student pairs to find all of the bigrams in the text \"The cat sat on the mat.\"\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work, check for misconceptions. A common misconception is that bigrams are found from non-overlapping pairs of tokens. Students who follow this approach will end up with only three bigrams: \"The cat\", \"sat on\", and \"the mat\". In reality, bigrams overlap and students should end up with five bigrams: \"The cat\", \"cat sat\", \"sat on\", \"on the\", and \"the mat\".\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Prompt student pairs to reflect on the process they used to find the bigrams in the text, formalizing this process as pseudocode.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work, encourage specificity. A useful prompt might be: \"Write your pseudocode as if I had to follow it with my fingers: where should I put them first, and where should I move them next?\" The goal of this exercise is for students to get a sense of the kind of code they’ll write when they find bigrams in Python.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Share the pseudocode you wrote. How would you describe the process of finding bigrams to someone else?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Prompt students to share their pseudocode. Strong responses will be specific, unambiguous, and actionable. Look for responses that relate to the \"sliding window\" approach students will learn next. In this approach, finding bigrams is the process of sliding a window of two tokens along a piece of text. As students share their pseudocode, make connections that set up the introduction of this approach.",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Finding Bigrams"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "41221ff0-d07b-4bd0-80a4-e1b4f3baea3f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "cec1aa52-d207-47fb-bb57-76f5d654d1a0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4818b809-b587-469c-8ff5-8a7c623f9066",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Guide students in writing a Python program that finds bigrams in a piece of text.",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Finding Bigrams in Python"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4818b809-b587-469c-8ff5-8a7c623f9066",
        "lesson_activity.key": "cec1aa52-d207-47fb-bb57-76f5d654d1a0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5d9c28b8-41c6-4c29-a3cc-6e8d6568e08c",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "In a few minutes, you’ll write Python code to find bigrams in a piece of text. Before you do, let’s get a sense of an algorithm you could use.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5d9c28b8-41c6-4c29-a3cc-6e8d6568e08c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "cec1aa52-d207-47fb-bb57-76f5d654d1a0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1c6f6e2c-b290-47a0-b81d-0dc0b0346e40",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the \"sliding window\" approach to finding bigrams. Let the animation run through both the bigram and trigram phases."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1c6f6e2c-b290-47a0-b81d-0dc0b0346e40",
        "lesson_activity.key": "cec1aa52-d207-47fb-bb57-76f5d654d1a0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6589fc30-3296-4c26-9a3b-8b5c00a8ec2e",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "One way to find bigrams in a piece of text is to create a \"window\" of two tokens. Then, as you capture each bigram you see in that window, slide the window over by one token. Continue this until you reach the end of the text.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6589fc30-3296-4c26-9a3b-8b5c00a8ec2e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "cec1aa52-d207-47fb-bb57-76f5d654d1a0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "84e11f77-38e6-49bf-b415-0a4ad8edbfbb",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Ask students to turn to a partner and compare the bigram and trigram phases of the animation.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "84e11f77-38e6-49bf-b415-0a4ad8edbfbb",
        "lesson_activity.key": "cec1aa52-d207-47fb-bb57-76f5d654d1a0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "935663cb-537e-4d32-998c-d15f225c2f8a",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Notice that the window then got one token wider to find trigrams. What is the same about how we find bigrams and trigrams? What is different?",
        "remarks": true,
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Listen for:\n- **Same:** The window starts at the first token, moves one token at a time, and stops at the end of the text. Each window position gives one n-gram.\n- **Different:** The window is 2 tokens wide versus 3. The trigram window also runs out of room sooner, so the same text gives one fewer trigram than bigrams (5 bigrams and 4 trigrams from “the cat sat on the mat”)."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "935663cb-537e-4d32-998c-d15f225c2f8a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "cec1aa52-d207-47fb-bb57-76f5d654d1a0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "266ce673-e51f-4e41-a3e0-9d2f8cdb63c2",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Motivate the introduction of tuples."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "266ce673-e51f-4e41-a3e0-9d2f8cdb63c2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "cec1aa52-d207-47fb-bb57-76f5d654d1a0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c39e37bf-84d8-4f12-a3f9-a75457fdfb2f",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Let’s keep our focus on bigrams as we get ready to write code. To store them in your program, you can use a new data structure.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c39e37bf-84d8-4f12-a3f9-a75457fdfb2f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "cec1aa52-d207-47fb-bb57-76f5d654d1a0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ef380f89-d26b-4e5a-ac11-e6705ed3c807",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *tuple*."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ef380f89-d26b-4e5a-ac11-e6705ed3c807",
        "lesson_activity.key": "cec1aa52-d207-47fb-bb57-76f5d654d1a0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "dbc5e7d0-b425-469d-9425-a64b4ecc83a8",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "A tuple is a data structure that bundles a sequence of values. Since a bigram, and an n-gram more generally, is a sequence of tokens, this data structure works well for representing bigrams.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "dbc5e7d0-b425-469d-9425-a64b4ecc83a8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "cec1aa52-d207-47fb-bb57-76f5d654d1a0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7962fe9b-6e1c-48ab-ba42-9881fae87dcf",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Describe the syntax for tuples in Python. Point out that, in Python, tuples are a collection of values with commas in between them. When students make a tuple, they should place parentheses around it to make clear that multiple values should be treated as a single one."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7962fe9b-6e1c-48ab-ba42-9881fae87dcf",
        "lesson_activity.key": "cec1aa52-d207-47fb-bb57-76f5d654d1a0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "dfd45e4f-6838-4c7f-ad69-d702253ce351",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "description": "A quick note on saying this word out loud. People pronounce “tuple” two ways: TUH-ple, like “couple,” and TOO-ple, like “scruple.” Both are correct, and you’ll hear both. In this class, we’ll say TUH-ple, but don’t be thrown if you hear the other one.",
        "remarks": true,
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students might ask about the difference between a tuple and a list. A list can also store a group of values in Python, but a list is \"mutable\" (its values can be changed, it can grow and shrink). A tuple is \"immutable\" (it cannot be changed and it has a fixed size)."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "dfd45e4f-6838-4c7f-ad69-d702253ce351",
        "lesson_activity.key": "cec1aa52-d207-47fb-bb57-76f5d654d1a0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "50878986-f3e5-4c03-9195-13ffc8881fc6",
      "position": 14,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Show students how they can implement the sliding window approach to finding bigrams in Python."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "50878986-f3e5-4c03-9195-13ffc8881fc6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "cec1aa52-d207-47fb-bb57-76f5d654d1a0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "27270834-891d-4a08-9299-640e464bb604",
      "position": 15,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Here’s one way to implement the sliding window approach we just discussed. This function is called `get_bigrams`, and it takes a list of tokens. Notice how we start with an empty list of bigrams. Then, we use a `for` loop to go through the positions in the list of tokens, except for the last one. The variable `I` is the position of the current token. Inside the loop, we build a window, a tuple, that includes the current token, `tokens[I]`, and the token after it, `tokens[i + 1]`. Then, we add that window to our list of bigrams. When the loop is finished, we return the list.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "27270834-891d-4a08-9299-640e464bb604",
        "lesson_activity.key": "cec1aa52-d207-47fb-bb57-76f5d654d1a0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5abc30da-cd1e-4c50-9404-f871078c0072",
      "position": 16,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Check students’ understanding with a discussion question.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Why does this program use `range(len(tokens) - 1)`?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Prompt students to consider why the program should not iterate to the last token in the list of tokens. Strong responses will suggest that it’s because the last token has no next token to build a window with. If some students mentioned this kind of case in their pseudocode from earlier in class, make a connection."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5abc30da-cd1e-4c50-9404-f871078c0072",
        "lesson_activity.key": "cec1aa52-d207-47fb-bb57-76f5d654d1a0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "524ae7dd-c4a8-4d90-914e-7a62c9bc9266",
      "position": 17,
      "properties": {
        "description": "The last token has no token after it, so it can’t be the start of a bigram. Python numbers positions starting at 0, so a list of 6 tokens, like “the cat sat on the mat,” has positions 0 through 5. If the loop went all the way to position 5, the window would ask for `tokens[6]`, which doesn’t exist, and Python would give us an error. By using `range(len(tokens) - 1)`, which is `range(5)` for this example, `I` goes from 0 to 4. The last window starts at position 4 and pairs “the” with “mat.” That’s also why 6 tokens give us 5 bigrams: there is always one fewer bigram than there are tokens.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "524ae7dd-c4a8-4d90-914e-7a62c9bc9266",
        "lesson_activity.key": "cec1aa52-d207-47fb-bb57-76f5d654d1a0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f503d845-1745-4e93-82d1-f98c2e5245af",
      "position": 18,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Show students how they can implement the sliding window approach when finding n-grams in Python no matter what the value of n."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f503d845-1745-4e93-82d1-f98c2e5245af",
        "lesson_activity.key": "cec1aa52-d207-47fb-bb57-76f5d654d1a0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "02d910d2-df18-43c4-9edd-b12e70752937",
      "position": 19,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Now, let's generalize the function so it works for any size n-gram. This function is called `get_ngrams`, and it takes a list of tokens and a number, `n`. We still start with an empty list, this time for the n-grams. Then, we use a for loop to go through the positions in the list of tokens where a full window fits. The variable `I` is the position where the current window starts. Inside the loop, instead of writing out `tokens[i]` and `tokens[i + 1]`, we use list slicing: `tokens[i:i + n]` grabs `n` tokens starting at position `i`. We want a tuple, like we had for bigrams, so we pass the slice to the tuple function. Then we add that window to our list of n-grams. When the loop is finished, we return the list.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "02d910d2-df18-43c4-9edd-b12e70752937",
        "lesson_activity.key": "cec1aa52-d207-47fb-bb57-76f5d654d1a0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "47b1e609-7d2a-4a68-9f3f-84cf0f6e971c",
      "position": 20,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Check students’ understanding with a discussion question.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Why does this program use `range(len(tokens) - n + 1)`?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Prompt students to consider why the program for n-grams also subtracts n in the range statement. Help students see how this helps identify each n-gram. For example, for a 6-token phrase “the cat sat on the map”, there are 4 trigrams — 6 tokens minus 3 (the value of n) plus 1."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "47b1e609-7d2a-4a68-9f3f-84cf0f6e971c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "cec1aa52-d207-47fb-bb57-76f5d654d1a0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "35d0c1ae-8a15-456d-9b53-d70fee2c4f38",
      "position": 21,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Now it’s time for you to implement this code yourself.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "35d0c1ae-8a15-456d-9b53-d70fee2c4f38",
        "lesson_activity.key": "cec1aa52-d207-47fb-bb57-76f5d654d1a0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a87ca610-eb56-488f-accd-128767272345",
      "position": 22,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Computing N-Grams"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a87ca610-eb56-488f-accd-128767272345",
        "lesson_activity.key": "cec1aa52-d207-47fb-bb57-76f5d654d1a0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ada3b7e9-3056-4696-8da3-e12820d9d36e",
      "position": 23,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Check Your Understanding"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ada3b7e9-3056-4696-8da3-e12820d9d36e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "cec1aa52-d207-47fb-bb57-76f5d654d1a0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "fc877a64-66f4-423f-a5ab-105f6f014078",
      "position": 24,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to work on Lesson 9, Levels 1–3.\n- **Level 1:** Students finish a program to find bigrams in a piece of text.\n- **Level 2:** Students finish a program to find n-grams of any size in a piece of text.\n- **Level 3:** Students complete a Check Your Understanding.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students complete their program, look for students who need additional support with writing their loop, constructing their window, or appending bigrams to the given list. Consider drawing a simple sentence on a sheet of paper, then illustrating how their loop should iterate over that sentence. Consider also prompting students to ask AI Tutor for help."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "fc877a64-66f4-423f-a5ab-105f6f014078",
        "lesson_activity.key": "cec1aa52-d207-47fb-bb57-76f5d654d1a0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8b31d319-b621-42bf-9b41-5061e7cc6381",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to answer the Question of the Day in their Unit Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8b31d319-b621-42bf-9b41-5061e7cc6381",
        "lesson_activity.key": "64f73fae-fa3b-45fe-ad8f-bad35b9bea34"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "51fda1f3-c8c0-451d-9407-1f26fd6606ea",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *You type “see you” and autocomplete offers three words. Where do those words come from, and how did autocomplete choose them?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students identify different ideas as to where autocomplete suggestions might come from. First, autocomplete has been trained on a great deal of text, including other people’s messages and the user’s own, and it remembers which words came after “see you.” Second, the suggestions offer the words that come next most often; “soon” is on the list of suggestions because “see you soon” is a commonly used phrase, not because the keyboard knows for sure what the user’s intent is. Students may say the phone is guessing, but help them see this as an algorithmic practice: an algorithm could determine what words are most likely to come next."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "51fda1f3-c8c0-451d-9407-1f26fd6606ea",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1890d83b-70a0-4550-a39d-a3fde43e6f79"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "fdf29e8e-94e5-45a4-8233-7d17da9ba25b",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "When I say a phrase like “see you”, you can complete the phrase with words you know often come next. A computer can do the same thing with the text that it’s been trained on. Today, you’ll see how those kinds of suggestions work.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "fdf29e8e-94e5-45a4-8233-7d17da9ba25b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1890d83b-70a0-4550-a39d-a3fde43e6f79"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a801073b-fccd-4258-8a9c-8fd2f4495125",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce this lesson’s career connection: a natural language processing engineer."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a801073b-fccd-4258-8a9c-8fd2f4495125",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1890d83b-70a0-4550-a39d-a3fde43e6f79"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5a57cdd6-289c-499d-bdc1-9371ff7aec01",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Today, you will play the role of natural language processing engineers building an autocomplete system. A natural language processing engineer builds software that reads, predicts, and generates language, like the autocomplete on a phone keyboard. They work at companies that make search engines, software applications, and chat assistants.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5a57cdd6-289c-499d-bdc1-9371ff7aec01",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1890d83b-70a0-4550-a39d-a3fde43e6f79"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "76b794dd-ef9e-4d74-8640-55a0182264ca",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "76b794dd-ef9e-4d74-8640-55a0182264ca",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1890d83b-70a0-4550-a39d-a3fde43e6f79"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a9b41d9b-0f1e-4cc4-afab-955225eaa136",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Students build a table of words and possible followers, using school announcements as training data. They then use this table to decide what word a model should predict comes after a given word.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the Be the Autocomplete Activity Guide and a die. Alternatively, have students use a virtual die on a computer or phone (a Google search for “roll dice” will show Google’s version of this tool).",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Be the Autocomplete"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a9b41d9b-0f1e-4cc4-afab-955225eaa136",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b8e23e06-6dc9-4317-8a2d-83e9b1e5e324"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d186f173-a2f3-444e-be58-a59df73ff953",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Imagine an autocomplete system that has been trained on school announcements. Everything it suggests is based on the data it’s trained on. Today, you’ll see how a computer might learn from this text data to predict the next word in a sequence.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d186f173-a2f3-444e-be58-a59df73ff953",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b8e23e06-6dc9-4317-8a2d-83e9b1e5e324"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4e49bc19-1224-4554-ab29-5bbbbb388688",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Give students 8–10 minutes to complete the Activity Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work through the Activity Guide, watch for these challenges:\n- If a word has multiple followers that are the same word, students might be tempted to write this word only once. Remind students that, if a word appears as a follower more than once, they should record it more than once in the follower table. The pre-filled example in the table demonstrates this.\n- Students might be unsure about what follows the last word in a sequence, like \"school\". For the purposes of this activity, the last word in a sequence does not have a follower.\n- Some students might treat “Attention” and “attention” as different words. For the purposes of this activity, uppercase and lowercase words are treated the same.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What were your predictions? Do they match those of your classmates?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Debrief the Activity Guide. Call on multiple students to share their predictions from the Activity Guide. Prompt students to notice where everyone had the same prediction (e.g., the prediction for \"will\") and where some students had a different prediction (e.g., the predictions for \"be\" and \"closed\"). Once students have noticed that some—but not all—predictions matched, guide the discussion towards the question of why. Strong responses will identify that randomness resulted in the different predictions. They may also note that all of the followers of \"will\" are the same (\"be\"), so no amount of random choice can ever choose a different word."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4e49bc19-1224-4554-ab29-5bbbbb388688",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b8e23e06-6dc9-4317-8a2d-83e9b1e5e324"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c62f4510-4ab7-496d-be6f-05e90d585c13",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "By making predictions as you did, you behaved like a small language model.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c62f4510-4ab7-496d-be6f-05e90d585c13",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b8e23e06-6dc9-4317-8a2d-83e9b1e5e324"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "275af03e-5cff-48e6-887e-7aadd7199a6c",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *language model*."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "275af03e-5cff-48e6-887e-7aadd7199a6c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b8e23e06-6dc9-4317-8a2d-83e9b1e5e324"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f034658d-73eb-41f8-94df-034a58fd2bc1",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Language models, like other models, rely on data. You saw this first-hand. First, you found patterns in some given text: which words follow which. Then, you used those patterns to predict what word should follow a given word. There’s a name for the kind of process you used to make a prediction—let’s learn it.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f034658d-73eb-41f8-94df-034a58fd2bc1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b8e23e06-6dc9-4317-8a2d-83e9b1e5e324"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "def55534-fa41-48fd-8f58-9e771af12dac",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *Markov chain*."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "def55534-fa41-48fd-8f58-9e771af12dac",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b8e23e06-6dc9-4317-8a2d-83e9b1e5e324"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b763ffae-e993-427d-87e2-037401801fff",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "A Markov chain is the general term for a process whose next step depends only on its last step. When you made a prediction about what word should come next, you relied only on the last word in a phrase.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b763ffae-e993-427d-87e2-037401801fff",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b8e23e06-6dc9-4317-8a2d-83e9b1e5e324"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e72d4989-3f35-48d6-9d39-94776ca91e41",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Students learn to implement a Markov chain-based autocomplete system in Python.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce a framework for building a Markov chain in Python.",
        "duration": "20",
        "name": "Markov Chains in Python"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e72d4989-3f35-48d6-9d39-94776ca91e41",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b8e23e06-6dc9-4317-8a2d-83e9b1e5e324"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "dd829953-670c-4a3b-be16-cd59ba16c987",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Let’s consider how to do what you did in the Activity Guide in Python. The table you made is like a dictionary: it maps individual words to lists of words that could follow them. In Python, you’ll write two functions. One is called `learn`, which adds tokens to the table—much like you did. The other is called `predict`, and it chooses a random token that follows a given token—again, much like you did.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "dd829953-670c-4a3b-be16-cd59ba16c987",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b8e23e06-6dc9-4317-8a2d-83e9b1e5e324"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7628680c-f7b0-483c-a124-e0d4ce3e9f6b",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce students to helpful syntax for building a dictionary of lists."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7628680c-f7b0-483c-a124-e0d4ce3e9f6b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b8e23e06-6dc9-4317-8a2d-83e9b1e5e324"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "23212477-8e10-4573-bc40-a3fbe34ed34b",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Before you start on the `learn` function, let’s look at some helpful syntax. Much like you did in the Activity Guide, you’ll want to create—and add to—lists in your dictionary.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "23212477-8e10-4573-bc40-a3fbe34ed34b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b8e23e06-6dc9-4317-8a2d-83e9b1e5e324"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "321d155e-eb2c-421d-8ad1-9540417fdb43",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "description": "To start a new list that contains a single follower, you can use the bracket syntax: `[follower]`. Then, once that list exists in the dictionary, you can add to it with append.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "321d155e-eb2c-421d-8ad1-9540417fdb43",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b8e23e06-6dc9-4317-8a2d-83e9b1e5e324"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6f1dc89b-29d5-43cd-bc8b-3611c4c4bdf8",
      "position": 14,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Go ahead and implement the `learn` function."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6f1dc89b-29d5-43cd-bc8b-3611c4c4bdf8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b8e23e06-6dc9-4317-8a2d-83e9b1e5e324"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "56b06ac2-5704-4545-9aa8-d51dad5c03c8",
      "position": 15,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Build a Markov Chain"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "56b06ac2-5704-4545-9aa8-d51dad5c03c8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b8e23e06-6dc9-4317-8a2d-83e9b1e5e324"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "dea02210-4623-4ed7-8752-33ec56e84fc8",
      "position": 16,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 10, Level 1.\n- **Level 1:** Students complete the `learn` function, which adds tokens and their followers to a dictionary.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work, look for:\n- Students who aren’t sure how to add tokens to the dictionary. Consider displaying the previous slide while students work, as a reference.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce students to `random.choice`."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "dea02210-4623-4ed7-8752-33ec56e84fc8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b8e23e06-6dc9-4317-8a2d-83e9b1e5e324"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a93f210e-af2b-4221-b3ec-acff782e3d90",
      "position": 17,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Next, you’ll work on the `predict` function. Much like you did in the Activity Guide, predict should select a random follower of a given token. A function that will be helpful to you is `random.choice`. This function chooses a random value from a given list.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a93f210e-af2b-4221-b3ec-acff782e3d90",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b8e23e06-6dc9-4317-8a2d-83e9b1e5e324"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0af40961-8a7f-401c-a895-53971c4faa16",
      "position": 18,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Build a Markov Chain"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0af40961-8a7f-401c-a895-53971c4faa16",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b8e23e06-6dc9-4317-8a2d-83e9b1e5e324"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5eb57a42-7696-4386-b628-f765348a39cf",
      "position": 19,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Check Your Understanding"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5eb57a42-7696-4386-b628-f765348a39cf",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b8e23e06-6dc9-4317-8a2d-83e9b1e5e324"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "afa84c86-459a-4651-b273-9d08de4919dc",
      "position": 20,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 10, Levels 2–5.\n- **Level 2:** Students complete the `predict` function, which returns a random follower of a given token.\n- **Level 3:** Students rewrite the `predict` function, updating it to return a list of five followers.\n- **Level 4:** Students write a `detokenize` function, using it to convert a list of followers into a single string.\n- **Level 5:** Students complete a Check Your Understanding.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work, look for:\n- Students who don’t check if their dictionary contains a given token before retrieving it from the dictionary. Prompt them to create a conditional that checks whether a token is a key in the dictionary before trying to access that token’s value.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use Level 5 as an assessment opportunity. Strong responses will be able to connect the role of training data to their model’s predictions, noting that a model trained on different data would generate different suggestions."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "afa84c86-459a-4651-b273-9d08de4919dc",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b8e23e06-6dc9-4317-8a2d-83e9b1e5e324"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "498cca92-4cc3-47f0-af79-0756241c9718",
      "position": 21,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to answer the Question of the Day in their Unit Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "498cca92-4cc3-47f0-af79-0756241c9718",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b8e23e06-6dc9-4317-8a2d-83e9b1e5e324"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4ae63d71-c961-4c6d-b4ed-4b4a00ee059d",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *A school newspaper uses a language model trained on past issues to generate new headlines. The model generated these two headlines. Which should be published?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students look at headlines generated by a language model and decide which should be published. Students should immediately recognize that headline A should not be published: it’s not grammatically correct. Use this as an opportunity to help students see that language models don’t always generate grammatically correct text. Students might suggest that headline B should be published, but encourage them to think critically about whether it’s always reasonable to publish. Strong responses will point out that there’s no guarantee that what the model has generated is actually true: fact-checking is needed to verify that the headline is accurate."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4ae63d71-c961-4c6d-b4ed-4b4a00ee059d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f758ab51-2011-4a5d-887d-463b35756b5a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "243b111d-7da3-4bb3-ae2c-304b72407894",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Sometimes, a program might generate grammatically wrong sentences. But even if the program generates grammatical sentences, it might sometimes write sentences that are not true. Style alone can’t tell us which is factual and which is not.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "243b111d-7da3-4bb3-ae2c-304b72407894",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f758ab51-2011-4a5d-887d-463b35756b5a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "cbf4e986-5929-439b-aace-1ead6b807805",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce this lesson’s career connection: a fact-checker."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "cbf4e986-5929-439b-aace-1ead6b807805",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f758ab51-2011-4a5d-887d-463b35756b5a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "be5249e3-6f5e-428d-82c7-85964036f040",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Today, you’ll play the role of fact-checkers at a school newspaper. A fact-checker verifies claims before they are published, tracing each one back to a source. Fact-checkers work at newspapers, magazines, media companies, and broadcasters. Increasingly, they check what AI systems say too.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "be5249e3-6f5e-428d-82c7-85964036f040",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f758ab51-2011-4a5d-887d-463b35756b5a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9c9d0337-fb88-4d0b-8e23-64da8d9089a7",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9c9d0337-fb88-4d0b-8e23-64da8d9089a7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f758ab51-2011-4a5d-887d-463b35756b5a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "dd5b714e-3768-4104-b190-ae486508cfad",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Help students see that AI doesn’t always generate truthful statements. Provide students an opportunity to practice the mechanics of fact-checking. By checking potential headlines against the newspaper’s actual record and spotting differences, students come to understand AI \"hallucinations\" and misinformation.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *hallucination*.",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Fact-Check Desk"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "dd5b714e-3768-4104-b190-ae486508cfad",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8db66155-7c3c-4235-a28e-225f381874cc"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a7f78b94-643e-4fc0-bff1-b60cb6cd12b0",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "When an AI system generates text that is presented as fact but isn’t actually true, we call that a “hallucination.” While language models can make headlines that look real, not all of them will be wholly true. Fact-checking is important to help address this issue.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a7f78b94-643e-4fc0-bff1-b60cb6cd12b0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8db66155-7c3c-4235-a28e-225f381874cc"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f2cd4bf6-7092-4844-8635-9523eeae866d",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Say a reporter let a language model draft this week’s headlines. In the next activity, you’ll need to verify whether each headline matches the record.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f2cd4bf6-7092-4844-8635-9523eeae866d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8db66155-7c3c-4235-a28e-225f381874cc"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b201b8ab-c413-41fd-b55d-7ff46fe4639b",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the Limits of Language Models Activity Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to complete the first section of the Activity Guide, pausing at the stop sign.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Watch for these student challenges:\n- Some students may focus on the plausibility of the headline. Help ensure that students are verifying headlines by checking it against the record of known facts.\n- Some students might wonder whether a headline that doesn’t appear in the record is definitely false. This isn’t necessarily the case: it’s possible that something might be true, but that the fact-checking might not have enough information to verify for certain that it is true.\n- Help students think through how students might be affected by incorrect information. For example, ask who would show up expecting the drama club to perform Into the Woods, and what they would think of the paper afterward.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Why do language models sometimes generate true statements? Why do language models sometimes generate false statements?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Prompt students to think about why a language model trained to generate headlines might sometimes generate true statements and sometimes generate false statements. Strong responses will identify factors that are involved in text generation: for example, students might note that if language models are trained on significant amounts of text data that is mostly true, then language models are likely to generate patterns of text that include those same true sequences of words. Strong responses will also notice that this behavior is based on statistics: language models generate text that is likely to be reasonable, but nothing about the language model itself is verifying that the words generated are all true.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *misinformation*."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b201b8ab-c413-41fd-b55d-7ff46fe4639b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8db66155-7c3c-4235-a28e-225f381874cc"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8670afa9-c54b-4ca6-980f-197508a497ed",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Once a made-up item is printed and shared, it is misinformation: false information shared as if it were true. We could say that hallucinations come from language models, but misinformation comes from us: we are the ones who printed it as if it were true.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8670afa9-c54b-4ca6-980f-197508a497ed",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8db66155-7c3c-4235-a28e-225f381874cc"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "96e4d4b1-7739-439d-9404-79e77eb4c4bc",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Students consider how natural language systems can fail. Students explore ways that adversarial actors might exploit natural language systems for their own gain.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Group students into pairs.",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Exploiting Natural Language Systems"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "96e4d4b1-7739-439d-9404-79e77eb4c4bc",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8db66155-7c3c-4235-a28e-225f381874cc"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2dd6e533-c849-4237-a3a1-9e61122f72e9",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "As you’ve seen, language models have limits and aren’t perfect. As a result, it’s often possible for people to take advantage of the limits of natural language processing systems. In the next activity, you and your partner will brainstorm some ways that people might take advantage or exploit these kinds of AI.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2dd6e533-c849-4237-a3a1-9e61122f72e9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8db66155-7c3c-4235-a28e-225f381874cc"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "97fb5147-5dab-4837-b92a-53762b97c44c",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to complete the Exploiting Natural Language Systems part of the activity guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work through the assignment, help them consider possible ways natural language systems can be exploited.\n- For search engines, ask students to think about how the search engine decides which pages to rank more highly. Students should observe that pages with the same keyword repeated multiple times rank more highly. While in general this can be a useful metric—students have seen examples where more frequent occurrences of words means the word is more important in the document—students should also observe that this could be exploited by including keywords many times on a page just to artificially boost search engine ranking.\n- For the comment filter, help students see that the model is trained based on specific user behavior: users reporting comments as spam. However, there’s no guarantee that users will be responsible or accurate in their reporting. As a result, it’s possible that users could coordinate to report comments they disagree with or that are made by people they dislike.\n- For the Naive Bayes tip line, remind students how a Naive Bayes classifier works: the classifier learns to predict the probability of a particular classification (for example, the probability that a tip is credible) given that particular words are present in the text. Students should imagine ways that this could be exploited: for example, a scammer could consider credible tips and include words that are likely to be present in credible tips, while still presenting misinformation.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *How could people take advantage of natural language AI systems? What should we do about it?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students reflect on the activity they just completed. Students should think about the various failure modes of the natural language systems they explored: strong responses might point out that if people can identify what an AI system is optimizing for (like particular keywords, or responding to particular user behavior), then they can use that information to gain an undue advantage. Students should brainstorm possible ways to address these limits—there isn’t a single right answer, but may answers will involve some kind of human oversight: while AI can make predictions, it’s ultimately up to people to be accountable for decisions, and that often requires reviewing AI’s generated output."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "97fb5147-5dab-4837-b92a-53762b97c44c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8db66155-7c3c-4235-a28e-225f381874cc"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ca25941c-1e36-4d87-8476-fefcaae988ca",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Check Your Understanding"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ca25941c-1e36-4d87-8476-fefcaae988ca",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8db66155-7c3c-4235-a28e-225f381874cc"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7e626150-5fdb-4e85-894a-5796b2b7269f",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 11, Levels 1.\n- **Level 1:** Students complete a Check Your Understanding.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use Level 1 as an opportunity to evaluate whether students understand some of the limits of natural language processing systems. Help students think about the ways that natural language processing systems can fail and the impact that has on how much people should trust these systems."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7e626150-5fdb-4e85-894a-5796b2b7269f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8db66155-7c3c-4235-a28e-225f381874cc"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9723d66a-80c0-4ed6-8f13-27d2de2d9769",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to answer the Question of the Day in their Unit Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9723d66a-80c0-4ed6-8f13-27d2de2d9769",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8b6ca1bd-0fc7-4cbe-b0e2-8a2746b7d44b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f033675b-ba21-4171-8849-7d3a4354cab5",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *You type “How do I prepare for” into a search engine and get three suggestions. Why do you think these are the suggested results?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students consider what might cause text models to make particular predictions. The goal is for students to reach the conclusion that predictions are based heavily on the data that a model was trained on. Strong responses will identify that the search engine’s prediction algorithm was probably trained on past search results: if a lot of people in the past searched for how to prepare for job interviews, the SAT, and driving tests, then those results become more likely to be shown as predictions in the future. If students have trouble surfacing these ideas, encourage them to think back to lessons earlier in the unit about sequences of words and the patterns that AI can learn from those sequences. For example, if “prepare for a job interview” is a sequence of words that often appears together, that’s a pattern that AI could learn."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f033675b-ba21-4171-8849-7d3a4354cab5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "cb5a5561-8719-4016-a2ad-6a50828d0c0a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d474d5d3-bbed-4925-990e-3d3a59271e28",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "The way AI behaves depends on the data used to train it. Today, we’ll look carefully at what that means in practice, and why we should be thoughtful about how AI is trained.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d474d5d3-bbed-4925-990e-3d3a59271e28",
        "lesson_activity.key": "cb5a5561-8719-4016-a2ad-6a50828d0c0a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "70c242db-98f6-45d8-b899-4922d93629aa",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "70c242db-98f6-45d8-b899-4922d93629aa",
        "lesson_activity.key": "cb5a5561-8719-4016-a2ad-6a50828d0c0a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "caf1009f-580d-4cff-b94c-5fe7ee05be6f",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Students explore a job application evaluation model and consider whether the decisions it makes are fair. Students consider the data used to train the model to predict how the model will behave and to evaluate the decisions that it makes.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define _corpus_. Mention its plural: corpora or corpuses.",
        "duration": "20",
        "name": "Job Applicant Predictions"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "caf1009f-580d-4cff-b94c-5fe7ee05be6f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "82d56c00-bfb3-41dc-b5a1-18ceef2eb75c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6f89ded2-785d-478f-833d-4129e72534ba",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "When training AI to work with text, the data usually takes the form of a text corpus. Today, we’ll explore a text corpus and analyze what an AI trained on it might do.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6f89ded2-785d-478f-833d-4129e72534ba",
        "lesson_activity.key": "82d56c00-bfb3-41dc-b5a1-18ceef2eb75c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e5dd2c57-bcea-4fee-bffc-4b6e5a8b08e6",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the scenario for today’s lesson. Students act as hiring managers reviewing job applications.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e5dd2c57-bcea-4fee-bffc-4b6e5a8b08e6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "82d56c00-bfb3-41dc-b5a1-18ceef2eb75c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b7bfdf61-d947-48d7-b3ec-d6ccdeafcd88",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Imagine that you were considering using AI to help you review job applications. The AI could be trained on a corpus of previous job applications. In the next activity, you’ll investigate: How would that work? Is it a good idea? What could go wrong?\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b7bfdf61-d947-48d7-b3ec-d6ccdeafcd88",
        "lesson_activity.key": "82d56c00-bfb3-41dc-b5a1-18ceef2eb75c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "12fbe5f7-974d-403a-bd26-28ebd2d4dadd",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Group students into pairs.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "12fbe5f7-974d-403a-bd26-28ebd2d4dadd",
        "lesson_activity.key": "82d56c00-bfb3-41dc-b5a1-18ceef2eb75c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "443a630b-0b00-4402-96d7-8a1014132330",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "In the next activity, you’ll explore what it might look like to use AI to review job applications. First, you’ll analyze a corpus of job applications to identify patterns that a machine learning model might learn. Then, you’ll see what kinds of predictions that machine learning model might make and decide whether you think the results are fair.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "443a630b-0b00-4402-96d7-8a1014132330",
        "lesson_activity.key": "82d56c00-bfb3-41dc-b5a1-18ceef2eb75c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8008c5c0-ca7e-44b2-a180-6e653514b3e2",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Distribute a copy of the Job Applications Activity Guide to each pair of students.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to complete the Job Applications Activity Guide in pairs.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Help students with the math, logic, and ethical reasoning as they work through the activity:\n- In the first part of the activity, students identify whether particular tokens appear more often in job applications for people who were hired or for people who were not hired. Help students see the purpose of the activity: students are identifying patterns that a machine learning model might also identify if it studied this particular text corpus. If students are unsure how to fill in the final column, ask them to compare the “Hired with Token” and “Not Hired with Token” columns: if the “Hired with Token” number is larger, that means the token shows up more often in people who were hired, so students should add a checkmark to the cell.\n- Students might be confused about the exclamation point. Remind students that punctuation can be a token, just like a word can be a token. Students should count how many hired applicants and not hired applicants had exclamation points in their application text.\n- Once students have identified the “signal” tokens that appear more often in hired applicants, students might need help figuring out how to use that information to make predictions. The prediction algorithm this activity uses is a simplification of what an actual machine learning algorithm might do: students should look at each application and count how many tokens are signal tokens (in this case, count how many times “east”, “responsible”, “team”, or “!” appear in the application text). That count becomes the candidate’s score, and the model prefers the candidate with the higher score.\n- Students might have questions about how to evaluate the fairness of each decision. There’s no one right answer here: these questions are inviting students to consider the ethical implications of using AI in practice. You can guide students by asking them to think about the reasoning for why the machine learning model preferred one candidate over the other. Then, ask students to think about whether that reason is a good reason to prefer someone in the hiring process.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Did the model make any decisions that you think were unfair? Why or why not?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students reflect on the activity they just completed and consider whether their model made any decisions they thought were unfair. There are a number of concerns students might identify. If students struggle to identify possible concerns with the model’s decisions, encourage students to look back at the data that the models were trained on. Help students think through how the training data influenced the predictions made by the model, inviting them to think about whether those predictions are reasonable.\n- With candidates 1 and 2, the model prefers the candidate who went to “River East High School”. Strong responses might identify this as a bias present in the model resulting from the data that the model was trained on, arguing that the model shouldn’t prioritize applicants just based on what school they went to.\n- With candidates 3 and 4, the model prefers the candidate with the longer response because the word “team” appears more often. Strong responses might identify that a long response shouldn’t automatically be considered a good one, but one weakness of many keyword-searching algorithms is that they implicitly advantage longer responses that have more opportunities for particular tokens to be present.\n- With candidates 5 and 6, the model prefers the candidate that used more exclamation points. Strong responses might identify that the model might have learned from its training data that exclamation points are an indication of enthusiasm for a job, but that including more exclamation points on its own shouldn’t be considered a reason to prefer a candidate. This is what is referred to in statistics as a “spurious correlation”: exclamation points might correlate with strong responses in the training data, but that doesn’t mean that including more exclamation points in a candidate’s application makes them a stronger candidate for a job.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8008c5c0-ca7e-44b2-a180-6e653514b3e2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "82d56c00-bfb3-41dc-b5a1-18ceef2eb75c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "07ac61a3-8512-4766-b03d-90785695b7f7",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "There are lots of places where AI might be helpful. But we should always think carefully about what kind of data is used to train models and what kinds of tasks we want those models doing.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "07ac61a3-8512-4766-b03d-90785695b7f7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "82d56c00-bfb3-41dc-b5a1-18ceef2eb75c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "15172f4d-dcf1-4dd6-9772-0c42793a78d2",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Students discuss questions related to the ethics of using AI in hiring. Students think about situations where AI could be helpful and situations where AI could create risks. They also evaluate what data could be used to make AI fairer.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Invite students to choose a topic they want to discuss. Divide the class into three groups based on which question each student chose. Give each of the three groups five to ten minutes to talk about their question and brainstorm ideas in response.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As groups discuss their question, help them think through ideas about AI and how it can be used appropriately. There isn’t a single right answer to any of these questions: encourage students to brainstorm and argue for their ideas.\n- For students discussing Question 1, strong responses might identify that data should be representative: to address concerns about possible biases, it should cover a variety of different demographic groups and backgrounds. Students might also identify other information that would help AI make a good decision: job descriptions, skills and qualifications, application questions. Students could also think about what data should not be included: for example, data like an applicant’s race or gender shouldn’t be used to rank candidates.\n- For students discussing Question 2, strong responses might identify applications of natural language processing that could be useful: text summarization could help recruiters identify important characteristics about each candidate, keyword extraction could help identify candidates who have the right skills and background for a job, text generation could help save time so that recruiters can spend more time face-to-face with candidates. Students might also point out that human reviewers have biases too, and AI could be trained to apply a standardized evaluation that could help to reduce some human inconsistency and bias.\n- For students discussing Question 3, strong responses might identify that AI evaluation could result in bias and discrimination, or note that the kinds of factors that an AI might prioritize (like particular keywords) don’t necessarily mean a candidate is better suited for a job. Students might point out that if AI is trained on an existing set of candidates, then it might overlook a good candidate who comes from a different background but who might still be qualified for a job. Students might also raise issues of accountability, pointing out that someone needs to be responsible for AI-assisted hiring decisions.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Once groups have had a chance to talk, bring the class back together. Call on at least one volunteer from each of the three groups to share what their group discussed.",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "AI Ethics Discussions"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "15172f4d-dcf1-4dd6-9772-0c42793a78d2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "82d56c00-bfb3-41dc-b5a1-18ceef2eb75c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9a00528e-a55c-44ab-b2f7-509baf479ad5",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "The questions you’ve thought about today are questions that are important everywhere that AI used. Thinking about the benefits and risks of AI as well as the data that it’s trained on can help us make better decisions about where and how AI should be used.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9a00528e-a55c-44ab-b2f7-509baf479ad5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "82d56c00-bfb3-41dc-b5a1-18ceef2eb75c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "149d7e89-c44b-46a1-94a2-04adc4b25cfa",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Discuss AI Ethics"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "149d7e89-c44b-46a1-94a2-04adc4b25cfa",
        "lesson_activity.key": "82d56c00-bfb3-41dc-b5a1-18ceef2eb75c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8e86d4db-cebf-48c0-9042-6cadc9519903",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Level 1.\n- Level 1: Students summarize the conclusions they reached in response to the ethics discussion."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8e86d4db-cebf-48c0-9042-6cadc9519903",
        "lesson_activity.key": "82d56c00-bfb3-41dc-b5a1-18ceef2eb75c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "132cd686-d174-4887-b1ac-9ac8c53a8aa8",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to answer the Question of the Day in their Unit Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "132cd686-d174-4887-b1ac-9ac8c53a8aa8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b64cef31-7b36-4137-bcc6-5b780f0cf49f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7db8bfa7-9683-4c62-a7b2-1d89910f6886",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the Text Generation Project Planning Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Warm Up"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7db8bfa7-9683-4c62-a7b2-1d89910f6886",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1a6eb83d-25c5-429e-9899-b8cc9f984aaa"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "da741a69-96bb-476c-a556-be79919b1bf7",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Introduce the Text Generation Project and its requirements.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the project requirements and project rubric.",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Choosing a Corpus"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "da741a69-96bb-476c-a556-be79919b1bf7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1a6eb83d-25c5-429e-9899-b8cc9f984aaa"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e16204f2-f166-4990-8df7-cd7f2c02e7f1",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "In this project, you’ll apply what you’ve learned in Unit 5 to build a language model that can generate text on its own. More specifically, you’ll build a bigram language model. This is a model that finds and learns from bigrams in a text corpus. It then uses those bigrams to generate text one token at a time. Like the Markov chains you saw in a prior lesson, a bigram language model predicts the next token based on the previous token.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e16204f2-f166-4990-8df7-cd7f2c02e7f1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1a6eb83d-25c5-429e-9899-b8cc9f984aaa"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1ea5e830-301f-4121-a440-c02cb613a46a",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "To build your model, you’ll first choose and contribute to a corpus of text. Then, you’ll finish a few functions to make your language model work. You'll make a function to tokenize your text and a function to train your model on bigrams of those tokens. You'll also make multiple functions to generate text with your trained model.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1ea5e830-301f-4121-a440-c02cb613a46a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1a6eb83d-25c5-429e-9899-b8cc9f984aaa"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9fe11fbd-b57c-42fc-9773-fd113506e527",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Invite students to ask questions about the project.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Share an example of the project in action. Highlight to students that their language model in this project will be similar to, but distinct from, the Markov chains they’ve previously implemented."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9fe11fbd-b57c-42fc-9773-fd113506e527",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1a6eb83d-25c5-429e-9899-b8cc9f984aaa"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "bff040e7-d381-46b4-88b0-d1303a2ea5cd",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Here’s an example of what you’ll build. You’ll get a corpus like this one: a corpus of single sentences with a common theme. Your model will then generate a new sentence, based on what it learns from the corpus.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "bff040e7-d381-46b4-88b0-d1303a2ea5cd",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1a6eb83d-25c5-429e-9899-b8cc9f984aaa"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "021388dd-1c63-46b0-8df3-248f075d0145",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Again, your model is similar to the Markov chains you’ve already created. But there’s one important difference in this project. Your model won’t just generate a fixed number of words. Rather, it will generate text until it comes to a natural stop, like the end of a sentence.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "021388dd-1c63-46b0-8df3-248f075d0145",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1a6eb83d-25c5-429e-9899-b8cc9f984aaa"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3ad32294-4509-4edc-8a65-9eea5eedaecb",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to complete Step 1 of their Project Planning Guide: Choosing a Corpus."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3ad32294-4509-4edc-8a65-9eea5eedaecb",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1a6eb83d-25c5-429e-9899-b8cc9f984aaa"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a75467da-1a40-4111-8922-afc701611914",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "The corpus you use will determine the kind of text your model will generate. Read about the corpora available to you, then choose the one you’d like to work with.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a75467da-1a40-4111-8922-afc701611914",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1a6eb83d-25c5-429e-9899-b8cc9f984aaa"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3dee1fff-9b8b-4b63-8697-3a7710b30249",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students read about the corpora, consider providing additional context about each corpus.\n- The sayings corpus contains short sayings students may be familiar with. Among them are \"The night is darkest just before the dawn,\" \"The truth is stranger than fiction,\" and \"The more the merrier.\"\n- The curiosities corpus contains questions about astronomy. Among them are \"Why does Mars look red?”, \"Why do planets orbit the Sun?”,  and \"Why do galaxies collide so often?\"\n- The invitations corpus contains invitations to birthday parties. Among them are invitations like \"Please come to my birthday costume party this weekend at the park!\" and \"Please tell everyone about my sixteenth birthday dinner on Friday evening at the arcade!\""
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3dee1fff-9b8b-4b63-8697-3a7710b30249",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1a6eb83d-25c5-429e-9899-b8cc9f984aaa"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5bcd32b8-d212-4542-b556-e7878194ddbb",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Students explore how their corpus influences their model’s output.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to complete Step 2 of their Project Planning Guide: Evaluating Corpora.",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Understanding Corpora"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5bcd32b8-d212-4542-b556-e7878194ddbb",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1a6eb83d-25c5-429e-9899-b8cc9f984aaa"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ea78c063-ea74-4459-8ddc-db7c7836b7c7",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Today, you’ll focus on understanding and contributing to the corpus you’ve chosen. Before you explore your own corpus, evaluate two hypothetical corpora to build your intuition about what makes a good corpus for your model.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ea78c063-ea74-4459-8ddc-db7c7836b7c7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1a6eb83d-25c5-429e-9899-b8cc9f984aaa"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "563c6b7d-52be-4cab-a623-e3b2413e4f56",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work, consider prompting them to notice the differences between the two corpora and guide them in making connections between those differences and a bigram language model’s performance.\n- Corpus 1 contains sentences that share a common grammatical structure: each sentence follows one of two patterns (e.g., \"Students must…\" or \"Students should…\"). Because every token appears in a consistent position, a bigram model has few ways to deviate from these grammatical structures.\n- Corpus 2 contains sentences with varied grammatical structures, and some tokens appear in more than one structure. Because a bigram model predicts each token using only the previous token, it can begin a sentence following one structure and, after reaching a shared token, continue following a different one. The result could be a new sentence that isn’t itself grammatically correct.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *How would a bigram language model’s behavior differ if trained on Corpus instead of Corpus 1? Explain.*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Guide students toward the realization that a bigram language model performs best when learning from a corpus of sentences with a shared grammatical structure. Strong responses will observe that Corpus 1’s sentences share a grammatical structure (e.g., all sentences begin with \"Students must\" or \"Students should\"). Strong responses will also connect observations about shared grammatical structures to the workings of a bigram language model (e.g., \"Because Corpus 1’s sentences follow a similar grammatical structure, a bigram model is less likely to combine tokens from two different grammatical structures that can’t work together.\"). If focusing on grammatical structure isn’t intuitive to some students, consider focusing on identifying more general patterns in the corpora. For instance, ask \"Which corpus’s sentences followed a shared pattern?\" Then, highlight that bigram language models will replicate the patterns found in their corpora: \"Remember that a bigram language model relies on following patterns in text data. In which corpus is that pattern clearer?\" Once students understand that a bigram language model works best with a corpus of sentences that follow a shared pattern, continue on.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Emphasize the key point from the prior discussion."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "563c6b7d-52be-4cab-a623-e3b2413e4f56",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1a6eb83d-25c5-429e-9899-b8cc9f984aaa"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a68558de-4bb4-4ba9-9cb9-c07cdf428306",
      "position": 14,
      "properties": {
        "description": "A bigram model can only be as consistent as the corpus it learns from. Keep this in mind as you explore your corpus and contribute your own sentences to it.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a68558de-4bb4-4ba9-9cb9-c07cdf428306",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1a6eb83d-25c5-429e-9899-b8cc9f984aaa"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "06dacb81-8a41-437b-bd3a-1c4bb1fb0196",
      "position": 15,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Students study the corpus they’ve chosen and identify common patterns in the sentences that compose the corpus. Students then contribute their own sentences to the corpus.",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Contributing to the Corpus"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "06dacb81-8a41-437b-bd3a-1c4bb1fb0196",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1a6eb83d-25c5-429e-9899-b8cc9f984aaa"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "12912e69-e9d9-426b-ac10-6fbdb3a0dbfd",
      "position": 16,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Now, it’s time to get more familiar with the corpus you’ve chosen. Study the sentences you see in the corpus and try to identify the pattern that most of them follow. Then, add your own sentences to the corpus so that your model will have your influence when it generates text.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "12912e69-e9d9-426b-ac10-6fbdb3a0dbfd",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1a6eb83d-25c5-429e-9899-b8cc9f984aaa"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "68f64ace-65da-4fcc-a3fb-7b889afad07b",
      "position": 17,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Text Generation Project"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "68f64ace-65da-4fcc-a3fb-7b889afad07b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1a6eb83d-25c5-429e-9899-b8cc9f984aaa"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f28ea264-9a93-4b9e-bf21-f19de46865d0",
      "position": 18,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 13, Level 1.\n- **Level 1:** Students study the pattern of sentences in their chosen text corpus. In their Project Planning Guide, they document the patterns they find in their own words. Then, they contribute their own sentences to the text corpus that follow the patterns they’ve found.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work on Level 1, look for:\n- Students who could use additional support in identifying the patterns in their corpus. Guide students in looking at how sentences begin, continue, and end. Point out common verbs and the way they are used. If students are familiar with grammatical functions such as subject, object, etc., guide them in spotting where these appear in sentences."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f28ea264-9a93-4b9e-bf21-f19de46865d0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1a6eb83d-25c5-429e-9899-b8cc9f984aaa"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f7a7b986-a872-4554-8568-31d163350bc7",
      "position": 19,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Prompt students to complete the Day 1 Reflection section in their Project Planning Guide.",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Wrap Up"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f7a7b986-a872-4554-8568-31d163350bc7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1a6eb83d-25c5-429e-9899-b8cc9f984aaa"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ddcf0cc8-86fb-46f2-96de-d6844e4e8f1e",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Place students in groups of two to three. Prompt them to discuss the question on the slide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What pattern did you find in your corpus? How do your contributions follow this pattern?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Prompt students to compare their understandings of the sentence patterns in their corpus, and to get feedback from their peers on how well their contributions match this pattern.",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Warm Up",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If time allows, consider grouping students with others who are using the same corpus."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ddcf0cc8-86fb-46f2-96de-d6844e4e8f1e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fa347b57-c53e-4729-abf2-fa7c4aa57b13"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "41e98311-05ab-46c3-b086-90bb23c24cda",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Guide students through implementing their bigram language model.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce students to the functions that compose their model, and how they work together.",
        "duration": "35",
        "name": "Text Generation Project",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Consider inviting students to open their laptops and navigate to Lesson 13, Level 2, to follow along with this diagram in the main function."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "41e98311-05ab-46c3-b086-90bb23c24cda",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fa347b57-c53e-4729-abf2-fa7c4aa57b13"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "79d815fc-57dd-4c5a-b397-ca6fcb9bea61",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Today you’ll focus on writing the functions that make your model work. There are four of them: `tokenize`, `learn`, `generate`, and `detokenize`. In this diagram, you can see how these functions work together. Let’s walk through it.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "79d815fc-57dd-4c5a-b397-ca6fcb9bea61",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fa347b57-c53e-4729-abf2-fa7c4aa57b13"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f8ee128b-f292-4810-b25f-37d68aa8af1d",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "First, notice model. In your program, you’ll see a variable called `model` at the top. Every function has access to it. It’s a dictionary that records which tokens can follow which. At first, it’s an empty dictionary. The `tokenize` and `learn` functions add tokens to model.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f8ee128b-f292-4810-b25f-37d68aa8af1d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fa347b57-c53e-4729-abf2-fa7c4aa57b13"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3dc970a3-f1b5-4bd9-bca8-2765784b2113",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Once you’ve built up model, it’s time to generate text. The `generate` and `detokenize` functions work together, using the model to build up a list of tokens that form a new sentence.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3dc970a3-f1b5-4bd9-bca8-2765784b2113",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fa347b57-c53e-4729-abf2-fa7c4aa57b13"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f37a1f4c-e44f-4fe6-9302-3ae3d84c4688",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Allow students to ask questions about the structure of the program they’ll build."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f37a1f4c-e44f-4fe6-9302-3ae3d84c4688",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fa347b57-c53e-4729-abf2-fa7c4aa57b13"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4b7530e7-67ae-4364-823e-5f279d8951da",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "It’s time to start working on these functions. Aim to finish most of them today, but don’t worry if you need more time. We’ll continue this work next class, too.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4b7530e7-67ae-4364-823e-5f279d8951da",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fa347b57-c53e-4729-abf2-fa7c4aa57b13"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c1b6573f-e3ca-41d0-9b39-a5a1a22960ac",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Text Generation Project"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c1b6573f-e3ca-41d0-9b39-a5a1a22960ac",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fa347b57-c53e-4729-abf2-fa7c4aa57b13"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "dbfbaeb6-4df7-4e55-9c14-28b3a01eabd1",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 13, Levels 2–5.\n- **Level 2:** Students complete the `tokenize` function. They write the function so that words and punctuation are tokenized separately.\n- **Level 3:** Students complete the `learn` function. They add tokens, and those that follow them, to the model dictionary.\n- **Level 4:** Students complete the `generate` function. They use model to randomly choose a new token based on the current token.\n- **Level 5:** Students complete the `detokenize` function. They combine the tokens from generate into a readable string.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work, look for:\n- **Level 2:** Students who don’t tokenize words and punctuation separately. Remind them about the regular expression they learned about earlier in the unit, which can do so for them.\n- **Level 3:** Students who aren’t sure how to associate a token with a new list of tokens, or how to add a token to an existing list. Prompt them to ask AI Tutor for syntax help, or remind them of the syntax yourself.\n- **Level 4:** Students who aren’t sure how to generate new tokens until reaching the end of a sentence. Point out that the model can know it should stop generating text when the previous token it generated does not have any tokens that could follow it—in other words, when the most recently generated token is not itself a key in model. Consider also facilitating the class-wide discussion about end-of-sequence tokens based on the following slide.\n- **Level 5:** Students whose corpus includes sentences with varied punctuation. By default, a student’s corpus includes only one punctuation character (\".\", \"?\" or \"!\"). However, if a student has added additional sentences that end with other punctuation, they’ll need to account for that in their `detokenize` function.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If some students finish these levels sooner than expected, invite them to continue contributing sentences to their corpus. How does what they add to their corpus change what their model generates?"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "dbfbaeb6-4df7-4e55-9c14-28b3a01eabd1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fa347b57-c53e-4729-abf2-fa7c4aa57b13"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "24662983-df59-4dbf-b4bb-b7678c01e674",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Once a critical mass of students are working on the `generate` function, consider pausing and exploring approaches to generating text until reaching the end of a sentence.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Imagine a computer generated this sentence, token by token. How do you think it knew when to stop?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Prompt students to consider logical ways a model could end its generation of tokens. Strong responses will mention an idea along these lines: a model could continue generating text until it reaches a certain, special token (e.g., the exclamation mark), at which point it knows it should stop. If students have difficulty identifying this approach, prompt them to envision what a model \"sees.\" For example: \"Imagine you’re the model, and you want to generate a sentence. All you can see is the last token you’ve generated. What information could you gather from that token to know whether you should continue?\".\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce how to terminate generation: an end-of-sequence token."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "24662983-df59-4dbf-b4bb-b7678c01e674",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fa347b57-c53e-4729-abf2-fa7c4aa57b13"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "19f23c02-45c0-4a6b-a50a-a9a0a126be6c",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "One way to terminate generation is with what’s called an \"end-of-sequence token.\" This is a special token that tells a model it is the last token it should generate. Usually, an end-of-sequence token is a special character you can’t see. But because, in this project, you’re generating single sentences, the end-of-sequence token could be a punctuation mark that all of your sentences end with.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "19f23c02-45c0-4a6b-a50a-a9a0a126be6c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fa347b57-c53e-4729-abf2-fa7c4aa57b13"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "53010b92-9a6c-4616-862c-b2d67374057b",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to continue working, applying what they’ve learned about ways to terminate generation."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "53010b92-9a6c-4616-862c-b2d67374057b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fa347b57-c53e-4729-abf2-fa7c4aa57b13"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "81169acf-07a4-424b-9a35-3c69a2a9577d",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Prompt students to complete the Day 2 Reflection section in their Project Planning Guide.",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Wrap Up"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "81169acf-07a4-424b-9a35-3c69a2a9577d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fa347b57-c53e-4729-abf2-fa7c4aa57b13"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ab2e1e25-ab4a-435d-bc5b-0a1c560fa73d",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** With a partner, evaluate these generations from a bigram model. Then, share how you evaluated them.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Gather ideas about how to qualitatively evaluate the outputs of a generative model. Strong responses will notice that one generation—\"The more the dawn\"—is grammatically incorrect, and so is undesirable. Another generation is grammatically correct, but not factual: \"Why does Europa have a black hole?\". Another generation—\"Please help me celebrate turning ten!\"—is grammatically correct and seems plausible, but could also be construed as somewhat plain. There isn’t a single correct answer in this discussion. Rather, the goal is to surface the many ways that the output of a generative model could be evaluated.",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Warm Up"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ab2e1e25-ab4a-435d-bc5b-0a1c560fa73d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e172c602-6411-4f5c-8f05-40b6923ee9a7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "50864f30-d360-4f96-adaf-4a1fa4dcfeee",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Unlike the models you’ve seen so far, there’s no one correct answer we expect from generative models. Rather, we need to assess the output of a model along multiple dimensions, some of which may be subjective. Today, you’ll choose a few metrics to evaluate your own model on.",
        "remarks": true,
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "For those unfamiliar, consider sharing that Europa is one of the moons of Jupiter."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "50864f30-d360-4f96-adaf-4a1fa4dcfeee",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e172c602-6411-4f5c-8f05-40b6923ee9a7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8c6a2b51-2f0e-4759-8d46-39aefdf53307",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Prepare students to evaluate their model using metrics of their choice.",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Evaluating Generative Models"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8c6a2b51-2f0e-4759-8d46-39aefdf53307",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e172c602-6411-4f5c-8f05-40b6923ee9a7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "da8d5d3a-8f49-4d0b-8ff9-c81159efcc85",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Here’s an example evaluation of one of the generations you just saw. This generation is grammatically correct, but isn’t factually accurate: Europa doesn’t have a black hole. That said, if it were true, it would arguably be an intriguing question—and maybe we’d like for our model to generate intriguing questions like this one.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "da8d5d3a-8f49-4d0b-8ff9-c81159efcc85",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e172c602-6411-4f5c-8f05-40b6923ee9a7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "df59f84d-50fd-46ed-86df-c998a58e4f6d",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "When machine learning engineers evaluate generative models, they may define metrics that represent what they hope to see in the model’s output. These metrics will vary widely—they could be things like \"helpfulness,\" \"cautiousness,\" \"accuracy,\" and more.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "df59f84d-50fd-46ed-86df-c998a58e4f6d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e172c602-6411-4f5c-8f05-40b6923ee9a7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "11e30ad0-28c4-4edf-afc9-248e7a771c81",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to identify metrics for their model in their Project Planning Guide."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "11e30ad0-28c4-4edf-afc9-248e7a771c81",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e172c602-6411-4f5c-8f05-40b6923ee9a7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "db012e19-2c13-4d12-ae19-afb68cf9c2e2",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Consider metrics you’d like to evaluate your model on. Then, write them down in your Project Planning Guide. You don’t need to have a rigorous definition of your metric. Just a sense of how you’d answer \"Yes\" or \"No\" if you asked \"Does this generation include what you’re looking for?\" is enough.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "db012e19-2c13-4d12-ae19-afb68cf9c2e2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e172c602-6411-4f5c-8f05-40b6923ee9a7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "bb95b593-78cf-4c72-8ecd-6ffafcd23b40",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Guide students in completing their text generation project and evaluating their model.",
        "duration": "20",
        "name": "Text Generation Project"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "bb95b593-78cf-4c72-8ecd-6ffafcd23b40",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e172c602-6411-4f5c-8f05-40b6923ee9a7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5756b12e-b2df-4ab8-af33-6fb700682266",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "It’s time to finish your text generation project. Once you get to Level 6, you’ll evaluate your model using the metrics you’ve added to your Project Planning Guide.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5756b12e-b2df-4ab8-af33-6fb700682266",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e172c602-6411-4f5c-8f05-40b6923ee9a7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8182b882-9241-4564-8953-a94baea38705",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Check Your Understanding"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8182b882-9241-4564-8953-a94baea38705",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e172c602-6411-4f5c-8f05-40b6923ee9a7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f5bc33aa-4049-4e77-a96a-fac75cf82be0",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 13, Levels 2–7.\n- **Level 2:** Students complete the `tokenize` function. They write the function so that words and punctuation are tokenized separately.\n- **Level 3:** Students complete the `learn` function. They add tokens, and those that follow them, to the model dictionary.\n- **Level 4:** Students complete the `generate` function. They use model to randomly choose a new token based on the current token.\n- **Level 5:** Students complete the `detokenize` function. They combine the tokens from generate into a readable string.\n- **Level 6:** Students evaluate their model using the metrics they’ve added to their Project Planning Guide.\n- **Level 7:** Students answer a Check Your Understanding.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work, look for:\n- **Level 2:** Students who don’t tokenize words and punctuation separately. Remind them about the regular expression they learned about earlier in the unit, which can do so for them.\n- **Level 3:** Students who aren’t sure how to associate a token with a new list of tokens, or how to add a token to an existing list. Prompt them to ask AI Tutor for syntax help, or remind them of the syntax yourself.\n- **Level 4:** Students who aren’t sure how to generate new tokens until reaching the end of a sentence. Point out that the model can know it should stop generating text when the previous token it generated does not have any tokens that could follow it—in other words, when the most recently generated token is not itself a key in model. Consider also facilitating the class-wide discussion about end-of-sequence tokens based on the following slide.\n- **Level 5:** Students whose corpus includes sentences with varied punctuation. By default, a student’s corpus includes only one punctuation character (\".\", \"?\" or \"!\"). However, if a student has added additional sentences that end with other punctuation, they’ll need to account for that in their `detokenize` function.\n- **Level 6:** Students who aren’t sure how to evaluate their model. Guide them in generating a sentence with their model. Then, ask them whether that generation meets any of the criteria they established in their Project Planning Guide."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f5bc33aa-4049-4e77-a96a-fac75cf82be0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e172c602-6411-4f5c-8f05-40b6923ee9a7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "089a222c-71b3-48b7-bfd7-b45f9fab45d7",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Wrap Up"
      },
